const EVENTS = [{"name":"Formation of the Sun and solar nebula","ma":4567,"kind":"formation","emoji":"☀️","blurb":"The oldest solids in the Solar System, calcium-aluminium-rich inclusions in meteorites, crystallized 4.567 billion years ago as a collapsing cloud of gas and dust ignited a new star. Everything on Earth is built from atoms forged before this moment.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Hadean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Accretion of Earth from the solar nebula","ma":4540,"kind":"formation","emoji":"🌍","blurb":"Earth grew in a few tens of millions of years as countless rocky planetesimals collided and merged in the disk around the young Sun, releasing enough heat to keep the growing planet partly molten. Radiometric dating of meteorites pins the Solar System's birth at about 4.567 billion years, with Earth largely assembled by ~4.54 billion.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Hadean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Core formation and differentiation","ma":4530,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"⚙️","blurb":"Molten iron and nickel drained downward through the magma ocean to form Earth's metallic core, while lighter silicates floated up to become the mantle. This chemical sorting also seeded the geodynamo that would eventually shield the planet with a magnetic field.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Hadean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Giant impact of Theia forms the Moon","ma":4500,"kind":"impact","emoji":"🌙","blurb":"A Mars-sized protoplanet named Theia slammed into the proto-Earth, vaporizing and ejecting a colossal disk of debris that coalesced into the Moon within perhaps a century. The collision reset Earth's spin, tilted its axis, and left the two bodies isotopically nearly identical.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Hadean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Magma ocean world","ma":4500,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🌋","blurb":"In the aftermath of accretion and the Moon-forming impact, Earth's entire surface was a glowing sea of molten silicate rock hundreds of kilometres deep, glowing red under a sky of rock vapour. As it slowly radiated heat away, the densest metal sank to form the core.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Hadean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Jack Hills zircons: oldest known Earth minerals","ma":4400,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"💎","blurb":"Tiny, durable zircon crystals eroded into the sandstones of the Jack Hills, Western Australia, date to as old as 4.404 billion years, the most ancient fragments of Earth ever found. Their oxygen isotopes hint that liquid water and a cool crust existed startlingly early.","importance":5,"lat":-26.1,"lng":116.9,"era":"Hadean","plat":-26.1,"plng":116.9},{"name":"Condensation of the first oceans","ma":4400,"kind":"ocean","emoji":"🌊","blurb":"As the magma ocean crusted over and the steamy atmosphere cooled below the boiling point of water, torrential rains fell for ages and filled the first global oceans. The Jack Hills zircons suggest surface water may have existed by 4.4 billion years ago.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Hadean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"A reducing steam-and-CO2 atmosphere","ma":4400,"kind":"atmosphere","emoji":"🌫️","blurb":"The early Hadean air was a dense, oxygen-free shroud of water vapour, carbon dioxide, and nitrogen belched from volcanoes, with essentially no free O2. A powerful greenhouse kept the surface warm despite a Sun some 25-30% fainter than today.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Hadean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Delivery of water and organics by impactors","ma":4300,"kind":"impact","emoji":"💧","blurb":"Carbon-rich chondritic asteroids, and possibly comets, delivered a portion of Earth's water and its first organic molecules as they crashed into the young planet. The isotopic fingerprint of Earth's water most closely matches these carbonaceous meteorites.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Hadean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Earth's magnetic field switches on","ma":4200,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🧩","blurb":"Magnetized Jack Hills zircons suggest a geodynamo, and thus a protective magnetic field, may have existed by ~4.2 billion years ago. Such a field would have helped shield the atmosphere and any nascent chemistry from the solar wind.","importance":3,"lat":-26.1,"lng":116.9,"era":"Hadean","plat":-26.1,"plng":116.9},{"name":"LUCA — the last universal common ancestor","ma":4200,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🌳","blurb":"Every living thing traces back to a single ancestral population, a heat-loving, hydrogen-eating cell that already had DNA, ribosomes, ATP synthase and a working genetic code. Molecular clocks place LUCA somewhere around 3.5-4.0 billion years ago, well before the two great domains split apart.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"EoArchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Acasta Gneiss forms — Earth's oldest known rock","ma":4030,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🪨","blurb":"Felsic gneisses in Canada's Slave Craton preserve zircon crystallization ages of about 4.03 billion years, making them the oldest intact rock yet found on Earth. They are metamorphosed granitoids, proof that continental-type crust already existed within 500 million years of the planet's birth.","importance":5,"lat":65.17,"lng":-115.55,"era":"EoArchean","plat":65.17,"plng":-115.55},{"name":"Late Heavy Bombardment (debated spike)","ma":4000,"kind":"impact","emoji":"☄️","blurb":"Around 4.1-3.8 billion years ago a barrage of asteroids and comets may have hammered the inner planets, cratering the Moon and resurfacing the young Earth. Whether this was a distinct spike or simply the tail end of steadily declining bombardment is still fiercely debated.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Hadean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Onset of plate-tectonic-like crust recycling","ma":4000,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"🪨","blurb":"By the close of the Hadean, patches of buoyant granitic continental crust had formed and were being reworked, though whether modern-style plate tectonics operated this early remains uncertain. The chemistry of the oldest zircons records repeated melting and remelting.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Hadean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Nuvvuagittuq greenstone belt — possible oldest crust","ma":3900,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🗿","blurb":"Rocks on the eastern shore of Hudson Bay in Quebec have been controversially dated to as old as 4.28 billion years, which would make them Earth's oldest crust; more conservative estimates place them near 3.8 billion. They host putative tube-and-filament microfossils in banded iron formation.","importance":4,"lat":58.3,"lng":-77.7,"era":"EoArchean","plat":58.3,"plng":-77.7},{"name":"Late Heavy Bombardment (debated spike in impacts)","ma":3900,"kind":"impact","emoji":"☄️","blurb":"Lunar melt-rock ages once suggested a cataclysmic surge of asteroid impacts around 3.9 billion years ago that scarred the young Earth and Moon. Recent isotopic work argues instead for a smoothly declining tail of accretion, so whether a discrete 'bombardment' happened at all is now genuinely contested.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"EoArchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Isua supracrustal belt deposited","ma":3800,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🏔️","blurb":"This 3.7-3.8-billion-year-old greenstone belt in southwest Greenland contains pillow lavas and banded iron formations that formed on an ancient seafloor. Its rocks carry carbon-isotope signatures many researchers read as chemical fingerprints of very early life.","importance":5,"lat":65.1,"lng":-50,"era":"EoArchean","plat":65.1,"plng":-50},{"name":"Origin of life (abiogenesis)","ma":3800,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🧬","blurb":"Somewhere between roughly 4.1 and 3.7 billion years ago, self-replicating chemistry crossed the line into life, most likely at alkaline hydrothermal vents or in mineral-rich surface ponds where proton gradients and RNA-like molecules could take hold. It is the single most consequential event in the planet's history.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"EoArchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Anoxic ocean and iron-rich seas","ma":3800,"kind":"ocean","emoji":"🌊","blurb":"Archean oceans held essentially no free oxygen; instead they were laden with dissolved ferrous iron and would have looked greenish, beneath a hazy, methane-tinged, oxygen-free sky. Banded iron formations record this reducing chemistry long before oxygen accumulated.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"EoArchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Nuvvuagittuq / Isua carbon-isotope biosignatures","ma":3800,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"⚗️","blurb":"Graphite grains in some of the oldest sediments are isotopically light, depleted in carbon-13 in the way living cells fractionate carbon. If biological, these smudges are chemical ghosts of the earliest life, predating any recognizable fossil.","importance":4,"lat":65.1,"lng":-50,"era":"EoArchean","plat":65.1,"plng":-50},{"name":"First stable cratonic nuclei (Pilbara & Kaapvaal)","ma":3600,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"🧩","blurb":"Small, buoyant blocks of granite-greenstone crust like the Pilbara and Kaapvaal cratons stabilized as the first long-lived pieces of continent. These proto-continents would become the seeds around which later landmasses grew.","importance":3,"lat":-21,"lng":119,"era":"EoArchean","plat":-21,"plng":119},{"name":"Faint young Sun and a hazy greenhouse","ma":3500,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🌫️","blurb":"The early Sun shone only about 70-75 percent as brightly as today, yet Earth stayed warm and largely ice-free, likely because a thick blanket of carbon dioxide and methane trapped heat. Resolving this 'faint young Sun paradox' remains an active puzzle in Earth science.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"EoArchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Oldest confident stromatolites — Strelley Pool","ma":3480,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🥞","blurb":"Layered domes and cones in the Pilbara's Strelley Pool and Dresser Formations are widely accepted as microbial reef structures built by ancient bacterial mats. They are among the earliest clear physical evidence of thriving microbial communities on Earth.","importance":5,"lat":-21.15,"lng":119.4,"era":"EoArchean","plat":-21.15,"plng":119.4},{"name":"Dresser Formation hydrothermal hot-spring deposits","ma":3480,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"♨️","blurb":"The 3.48-billion-year-old Dresser Formation in Western Australia preserves geyserite and other hot-spring textures, evidence that microbial life inhabited terrestrial hydrothermal settings on land, not just the sea. It supports Darwin's 'warm little pond' as a cradle for early life.","importance":4,"lat":-21.13,"lng":119.4,"era":"EoArchean","plat":-21.13,"plng":119.4},{"name":"Rise of oxygenic photosynthesis","ma":2900,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🫧","blurb":"Cyanobacteria evolved the ability to split water using sunlight, releasing free oxygen as a waste product for the first time. This biochemical innovation would eventually rewrite the chemistry of the whole planet.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Neoarchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Deep-marine sulfate-poor, iron-rich oceans","ma":2900,"kind":"ocean","emoji":"🌊","blurb":"Archean seawater was rich in dissolved ferrous iron and very low in sulfate and oxygen, a chemistry utterly unlike today's oceans. This ferruginous state set the stage for banded iron formation whenever oxygen or oxidants appeared.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Neoarchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Oxygen oases in a still-anoxic world","ma":2800,"kind":"atmosphere","emoji":"🏝️","blurb":"Local patches of shallow water above cyanobacterial mats became transiently oxygen-rich even as the global atmosphere stayed essentially oxygen-free. Whiffs of oxygen appear in the rock record hundreds of millions of years before the atmosphere itself changed.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Neoarchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Widespread stromatolite reefs flourish","ma":2700,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🪨","blurb":"Layered, dome- and cone-shaped microbial reefs built by mat-forming bacteria spread across shallow Archean seas, leaving abundant fossil bioherms in the Pilbara and Kaapvaal cratons. They are the era's most conspicuous fossil evidence of life.","importance":5,"lat":-21,"lng":119.5,"era":"Neoarchean","plat":-21,"plng":119.5},{"name":"Craton stabilisation and continental growth","ma":2700,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"🌍","blurb":"Ancient continental nuclei such as the Pilbara, Kaapvaal, and Superior cratons thickened and cooled into rigid, long-lived keels. A large fraction of Earth's continental crust was extracted from the mantle during this interval.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Neoarchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Greenstone belt volcanism","ma":2700,"kind":"volcanic","emoji":"🌋","blurb":"Belts of pillowed submarine basalts, komatiites, and volcanic-hosted deposits were welded between granite domes as young crust assembled. The ultra-hot komatiite lavas erupted at temperatures rarely seen after the Archean, recording a hotter mantle.","importance":4,"lat":48.4,"lng":-81.3,"era":"Neoarchean","plat":48.4,"plng":-81.3},{"name":"Kenorland supercontinent assembly","ma":2700,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"🧩","blurb":"Accreting Neoarchean cratons collided to build one of Earth's earliest known supercontinents, Kenorland (also called Superia). Its assembly around 2.7 billion years ago clustered much of the young continental crust into a single mass.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Neoarchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Late Archean mantle superplume / crustal growth peak","ma":2700,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"🔥","blurb":"A worldwide pulse of komatiite eruptions, greenstone formation, and juvenile crust production around 2.7 Ga is often attributed to major mantle plume activity. It is one of the largest crust-forming events in Earth history.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Neoarchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Contested ~2.7 Ga eukaryote biomarkers reinterpreted as contamination","ma":2700,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"⚗️","blurb":"Sterane 'molecular fossils' once read as evidence for early eukaryotes and oxygen at 2.7 Ga were shown in ultraclean drill cores to be modern contamination. The claim for Archean eukaryotes and oxygenic photosynthesis at this age is now widely rejected.","importance":3,"lat":-22.3,"lng":119.7,"era":"Neoarchean","plat":-22.3,"plng":119.7},{"name":"Onset of major banded iron formations","ma":2500,"kind":"ocean","emoji":"🟥","blurb":"Oxygen produced by cyanobacteria reacted with iron dissolved in the still-anoxic oceans, precipitating alternating red-and-grey layers of iron oxide and chert onto the seafloor. These banded iron formations are the world's chief source of iron ore.","importance":5,"lat":-22.6,"lng":118.6,"era":"Neoarchean","plat":-22.6,"plng":118.6},{"name":"Peak of banded iron formation (BIF) deposition","ma":2480,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🟥","blurb":"Vast layered deposits of iron oxide and chert, like the Hamersley Basin of Western Australia, precipitated as oxygen reacted with dissolved ferrous iron in the seas and rained rust to the seafloor. These BIFs became the world's largest iron ore reserves.","importance":5,"lat":-22.3,"lng":118.5,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian","plat":-22.3,"plng":118.5},{"name":"Great Oxidation Event (GOE) begins","ma":2430,"kind":"atmosphere","emoji":"💨","blurb":"Free molecular oxygen produced by cyanobacteria began to accumulate irreversibly in Earth's atmosphere, permanently ending the deep-anoxic world and rusting the planet. Sulfur mass-independent fractionation vanishes from the rock record around this time, marking the atmospheric tipping point.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Loss of sulfur mass-independent fractionation (S-MIF)","ma":2400,"kind":"atmosphere","emoji":"🌫️","blurb":"The disappearance of anomalous sulfur-33 signatures in sediments records the moment atmospheric O2 rose above roughly 0.001% of modern levels, a chemical fingerprint of the newly oxygenated air. This is the single most cited geochemical marker of the GOE.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Ozone layer begins to form","ma":2400,"kind":"atmosphere","emoji":"🛡️","blurb":"As oxygen accumulated, ultraviolet light split O2 and forged the first stratospheric ozone, beginning to shield the surface from lethal UV radiation. This nascent shield made shallow-water and eventually land habitats far more survivable.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Huronian glaciation / first Snowball Earth","ma":2350,"kind":"climate","emoji":"❄️","blurb":"Three stacked glacial diamictites in the Huronian Supergroup near Lake Huron record Earth's first great ice ages, when rising oxygen destroyed the methane greenhouse and plunged the planet into possible global glaciation. Ice may have reached the equator between about 2450 and 2220 Ma.","importance":5,"lat":46.5,"lng":-82,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian","plat":46.5,"plng":-82},{"name":"Oxidative continental weathering ('red beds' precursors)","ma":2300,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🧱","blurb":"With oxygen in the air, rivers and rainwater began oxidizing iron and sulfur on land, altering paleosols and mobilizing new elements like sulfate and molybdenum into the oceans. Ancient soils switched from iron-loss to iron-retention profiles across this interval.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Lomagundi-Jatuli carbon isotope excursion","ma":2200,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"📈","blurb":"The largest and longest positive carbon-13 excursion in Earth history, lasting roughly 130 million years, recorded a massive pulse of organic carbon burial and a probable overshoot of atmospheric oxygen. Its abrupt end may have starved the biosphere back into low-oxygen conditions.","importance":4,"lat":-17,"lng":30,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian","plat":-17,"plng":30},{"name":"Assembly and rifting of early supercontinents","ma":2100,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"🗺️","blurb":"Cratons that had aggregated into early landmasses such as Kenorland began to rift apart during this era, reshaping ocean chemistry and nutrient delivery. Tectonic reorganization influenced the timing of both glaciations and oxygen swings.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Lomagundi-Jatuli carbon isotope excursion peaks","ma":2100,"kind":"atmosphere","emoji":"📈","blurb":"The largest positive carbon-isotope excursion in Earth history, when marine carbonates became enriched in carbon-13 as vast amounts of organic carbon were buried, a signature of surging photosynthetic productivity following the first great oxygenation. Recorded in rocks from Fennoscandia to Gabon.","importance":4,"lat":62,"lng":30,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":62,"plng":30},{"name":"Great Oxidation 'overshoot' and crash","ma":2060,"kind":"atmosphere","emoji":"📉","blurb":"After the Lomagundi excursion, oxygen and the biosphere appear to have crashed into a prolonged low-oxygen 'boring' interval, with organic-rich shales like the Zaonega and Francevillian formations recording the aftermath. Full oxygenation would not return for over a billion years.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Bushveld Complex intrusion","ma":2055,"kind":"volcanic","emoji":"💎","blurb":"A colossal layered igneous intrusion crystallised in what is now South Africa, forming the world's greatest reserves of platinum, chromium and vanadium. It records vast pulses of mantle magma pooling beneath a stabilising continent.","importance":3,"lat":-25,"lng":29.5,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":-25,"plng":29.5},{"name":"Vredefort impact","ma":2023,"kind":"impact","emoji":"☄️","blurb":"An asteroid roughly 10-15 km wide struck what is now South Africa, blasting the largest and oldest confirmed impact crater on Earth, originally over 250 km across. The deeply eroded scar still warps the rocks of the Witwatersrand goldfields.","importance":3,"lat":-27,"lng":27.5,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":-27,"plng":27.5},{"name":"Shunga event: giant organic carbon burial","ma":2000,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🛢️","blurb":"Enormous accumulations of organic carbon buried in the Onega Basin of Karelia produced 'shungite', a natural near-graphite rock, testifying to a biosphere already fixing carbon on a massive scale. Some deposits resemble ancient petroleum reservoirs a billion years before complex life.","importance":3,"lat":62.1,"lng":34.3,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":62.1,"plng":34.3},{"name":"Great Oxidation aftermath: atmospheric oxygen stabilises low","ma":1900,"kind":"atmosphere","emoji":"🌬️","blurb":"After the dramatic oxygen rise and Lomagundi excursion, atmospheric oxygen settled to a persistently low level, perhaps a few percent of modern, entering a long interval of environmental stability. This calm backdrop framed the slow emergence of the eukaryotic cell.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Gunflint chert deposited","ma":1880,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🔬","blurb":"Silica-rich sediments along Lake Superior entombed an exquisitely preserved community of microbes, later famous as the Gunflint microbiota that helped prove Precambrian life was real. The chert captures filaments, spheres and umbrella-shaped microfossils in stone.","importance":4,"lat":48.4,"lng":-89.6,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":48.4,"plng":-89.6},{"name":"End of the great banded iron formations","ma":1850,"kind":"ocean","emoji":"🧱","blurb":"The last of the giant Precambrian banded iron formations, including the vast Superior-type deposits of North America, precipitated as dissolved iron in the oceans was finally exhausted by rising oxygen. After this the seas turned from iron-rich to sulphide-influenced.","importance":4,"lat":47.5,"lng":-87.5,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":47.5,"plng":-87.5},{"name":"Sudbury impact","ma":1849,"kind":"impact","emoji":"🌠","blurb":"A giant bolide struck Ontario, excavating a crater whose molten melt sheet cooled to concentrate one of the richest nickel-copper ore bodies on the planet. Shattered debris from the blast is found hundreds of kilometres away.","importance":3,"lat":46.6,"lng":-81.2,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":46.6,"plng":-81.2},{"name":"Trans-Hudson Orogeny climax","ma":1820,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"⛰️","blurb":"A Himalayan-scale collision sutured the Superior and Hearne cratons across what is now central Canada, building towering mountains whose roots survive in the Canadian Shield. It was a key seam in the amalgamation of Columbia.","importance":3,"lat":54,"lng":-101,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":54,"plng":-101},{"name":"Assembly of Columbia (Nuna) supercontinent","ma":1800,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"🗺️","blurb":"Earth's first well-documented supercontinent finished assembling as ancient cratons collided along great mountain belts like the Trans-Hudson orogen, welding much of the planet's land into a single mass. It would remain largely intact for hundreds of millions of years.","importance":5,"lat":55,"lng":-100,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":55,"plng":-100},{"name":"Onset of the 'Boring Billion' stability","ma":1800,"kind":"climate","emoji":"😴","blurb":"Earth entered a remarkably quiet interval of stable climate, sluggish tectonics and modest oxygen sometimes called the Boring Billion. Beneath the surface calm, however, the eukaryotic cell was being assembled from bacterial parts.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"The Boring Billion begins","ma":1800,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"😴","blurb":"A ~1-billion-year interval of extraordinary environmental, climatic, and evolutionary stability sets in, with no major glaciations, no large carbon-isotope excursions, and slow biological change. Stable but low atmospheric oxygen and stratified, largely anoxic oceans dominate the planet.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Columbia (Nuna) supercontinent breakup","ma":1500,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"🌍","blurb":"The earlier supercontinent Columbia/Nuna finishes rifting apart during the early Mesoproterozoic, its fragments drifting before they would later reassemble. Prolonged tectonic quiescence and stable continental configurations characterize much of the era.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Mesoproterozoic anoxic-ferruginous oceans","ma":1450,"kind":"ocean","emoji":"🌊","blurb":"Oceans are strongly stratified with oxygenated surface waters over vast anoxic deep water that is iron-rich (ferruginous) with sulfidic (euxinic) zones along productive margins. This low-oxygen chemistry likely throttled eukaryote diversification for hundreds of millions of years.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Chloroplast endosymbiosis (primary plastid origin)","ma":1400,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🌿","blurb":"A heterotrophic eukaryote engulfs a cyanobacterium and retains it as a permanent organelle, founding the Archaeplastida and giving rise to red algae, green algae, and glaucophytes. This single event is the ancestral source of nearly all plastids and ultimately all land plants.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Persistent low atmospheric oxygen","ma":1350,"kind":"atmosphere","emoji":"🫧","blurb":"Atmospheric oxygen sits well below modern levels, plausibly only a few percent of present-day concentrations, held in a stable low state for the whole era. This oxygen ceiling is a leading explanation for why complex life stalled during the Boring Billion.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Grenville orogeny","ma":1100,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"⛰️","blurb":"A continent-scale collision welds Amazonia and other cratons onto Laurentia, raising a Himalayan-scale mountain chain whose eroded roots still form the basement of eastern North America. It was the climactic mountain-building event of Rodinia's assembly.","importance":4,"lat":45,"lng":-75,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":45,"plng":-75},{"name":"Bangiomorpha and the dawn of sex","ma":1050,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🧬","blurb":"The multicellular red alga Bangiomorpha pubescens in Arctic Canada shows differentiated cells, a holdfast, and spores, making it the oldest fossil with clear evidence of sexual reproduction. It anchors calibrations for the origin of complex multicellularity and eukaryotic sex.","importance":5,"lat":73,"lng":-80,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":73,"plng":-80},{"name":"Rodinia supercontinent assembles","ma":1000,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"🗺️","blurb":"Grenville-age mountain-building sutures nearly all continental crust into the supercontinent Rodinia, straddling the equator with Laurentia near its core. Its assembly built vast mountain belts whose later weathering would help reshape ocean chemistry.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Bitter Springs carbon isotope anomaly","ma":800,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"📉","blurb":"A large, prolonged negative excursion in marine carbonate carbon isotopes recorded in central Australia marks a rare disturbance to the otherwise placid Boring Billion carbon cycle. It likely reflects major reorganization of ocean chemistry as Rodinia began to break up.","importance":3,"lat":-23.5,"lng":133.5,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":15.25,"plng":112.78},{"name":"Rodinia begins to rift apart","ma":800,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"💥","blurb":"Mantle plumes and continental rifting start tearing Rodinia apart, opening new ocean basins and dramatically increasing the length of weatherable coastlines. This breakup set the stage for the runaway cooling of the Cryogenian Snowball Earths.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Late Tonian oxygenation stirrings","ma":780,"kind":"atmosphere","emoji":"🌬️","blurb":"Toward the end of the Tonian, geochemical proxies hint at the first rises in oxygen and nutrient availability that would culminate in the Neoproterozoic Oxygenation Event. The long chemical stasis of the Boring Billion begins to loosen its grip.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Approach to the Cryogenian","ma":730,"kind":"climate","emoji":"❄️","blurb":"As Rodinia's fragments cluster near the tropics, intense silicate weathering starts drawing down atmospheric CO2, edging the climate toward its first global glaciation in nearly a billion years. The stable warmth of the Boring Billion is ending.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Sturtian Snowball Earth begins","ma":717,"kind":"climate","emoji":"❄️","blurb":"The eruption of the Franklin Large Igneous Province drew down atmospheric CO2 and tipped Earth into its most severe glaciation, entombing the planet in ice from the poles all the way to the equator.","importance":5,"lat":72,"lng":-100,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":-2.25,"plng":138.71},{"name":"Rodinia finishes breaking apart","ma":700,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"🌍","blurb":"The supercontinent Rodinia rifted into fragments through the Cryogenian, opening new oceans and shallow shelves whose weathering helped both trigger the snowballs and later cradle the first animals.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Sturtian glaciation ends","ma":660,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🧊","blurb":"After roughly 57 million years - the longest ice age in Earth's history - the Sturtian snowball finally collapsed as volcanic CO2 built up to greenhouse extremes under the frozen crust.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Marinoan Snowball Earth","ma":650,"kind":"climate","emoji":"⛄","blurb":"A second global freeze locked the planet in ice for millions of years; its abrupt end at 635 Ma is recorded worldwide by distinctive 'cap carbonate' rocks draped over the glacial debris.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Marinoan deglaciation & cap carbonates","ma":635,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🌡️","blurb":"The Marinoan snowball ended in a runaway hothouse, and warm seas precipitated the globe-spanning Doushantuo/Nuccaleena cap carbonates that mark the base of the Ediacaran Period.","importance":5,"lat":30.8,"lng":111,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":41.03,"plng":44.71},{"name":"Neoproterozoic Oxygenation Event","ma":620,"kind":"atmosphere","emoji":"💨","blurb":"Atmospheric and oceanic oxygen rose sharply after the snowball meltdowns flooded the seas with nutrients, crossing a threshold that made large, energy-hungry animal bodies possible.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Gaskiers glaciation","ma":580,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🏔️","blurb":"A brief, geologically short glaciation - not a full snowball - immediately preceded the Avalon explosion of large Ediacaran organisms at Mistaken Point, hinting that climate upheaval primed the rise of complex life.","importance":4,"lat":47.2,"lng":-53.9,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":-56.95,"plng":-13.05},{"name":"Avalon explosion of Ediacaran biota","ma":575,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🌿","blurb":"On the deep, ash-buried seafloor now exposed at Mistaken Point, Newfoundland, the first communities of large, complex organisms - frond-like rangeomorphs - appeared, Earth's oldest known big multicellular life.","importance":5,"lat":46.6,"lng":-53.2,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":-57.12,"plng":-11.68},{"name":"White Sea Ediacaran assemblage","ma":558,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🪱","blurb":"Along Russia's White Sea coast, the classic Ediacaran fauna flourished - Dickinsonia, Kimberella, Spriggina - preserved with trackways showing that some of these creatures could move.","importance":4,"lat":66,"lng":40,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":-23.75,"plng":-100.54},{"name":"Rise of animal biomineralization (Nama assemblage)","ma":550,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🐚","blurb":"In the shallow reefs of Namibia's Nama Group, Cloudina and Namacalathus built the first mineralized skeletons, launching the biological arms race that would explode in the Cambrian.","importance":5,"lat":-25,"lng":16,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":-49.88,"plng":151.81},{"name":"Ediacaran ends / dawn of the Cambrian","ma":538.8,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"⏳","blurb":"The soft-bodied Ediacaran world gave way to the Cambrian as burrowing, shelly, mobile animals took over - the boundary defined by the first appearance of the complex trace fossil Treptichnus pedum.","importance":5,"lat":47.1,"lng":-52.9,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":-65.47,"plng":-40.36},{"name":"Base of the Cambrian / Fortunian Stage begins","ma":538.8,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"⏳","blurb":"The Precambrian gives way to the Phanerozoic, marked by the first appearance of the burrowing trace fossil Treptichnus pedum in Newfoundland's Fortune Head cliffs.","importance":4,"lat":47.1,"lng":-55.8,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":-65.54,"plng":-45.12},{"name":"Cambrian Explosion","ma":530,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"💥","blurb":"Over roughly 20 million years nearly every major animal body plan appears in the fossil record, as predation drives the sudden evolution of shells, spines, eyes, and complex food webs.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Chengjiang biota (Maotianshan Shales)","ma":518,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🐟","blurb":"A soft-bodied fossil Lagerstätte in Yunnan preserving the earliest known vertebrates, arthropods, and worms in exquisite detail from Cambrian Stage 3.","importance":5,"lat":24.7,"lng":102.9,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":-20.86,"plng":97.69},{"name":"Sirius Passet biota","ma":518,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"❄️","blurb":"An early Cambrian soft-bodied deposit in remote North Greenland preserving giant Kerygmachela and other stem-arthropods, complementing Chengjiang in the Northern Hemisphere.","importance":3,"lat":82.8,"lng":-42.2,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":-9.91,"plng":-113.83},{"name":"Emu Bay Shale","ma":513,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"👁️","blurb":"This South Australian Lagerstätte preserves fossilized compound eyes with thousands of lenses, revealing that Cambrian predators like Anomalocaris already had acute vision.","importance":3,"lat":-35.6,"lng":137.5,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":-4.67,"plng":135.9},{"name":"Burgess Shale","ma":508,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🦐","blurb":"A landslide-buried seafloor community high in the Canadian Rockies preserving Opabinia, Hallucigenia, Wiwaxia, and Anomalocaris, the richest window onto Cambrian animal life.","importance":5,"lat":51.4,"lng":-116.5,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":51.4,"plng":-116.5},{"name":"Great Ordovician Biodiversification Event (GOBE)","ma":470,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🌊","blurb":"Marine biodiversity roughly tripled as filter-feeders, reef-builders, and plankton radiated explosively, filling the seas with the richest ecosystems yet seen.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"First land plants (cryptospores)","ma":470,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🌱","blurb":"Tiny liverwort-grade plants colonize damp ground, leaving behind the oldest cryptospores in Middle Ordovician rocks of Argentina, the first green tinge on the continents.","importance":5,"lat":-22.5,"lng":-65,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":-29.42,"plng":-164.53},{"name":"Ordovician meteorite bombardment","ma":466,"kind":"impact","emoji":"☄️","blurb":"The break-up of a large asteroid in the belt showered Earth with L-chondrite meteorites, recorded as fossil meteorites in Ordovician limestone quarries in Sweden.","importance":2,"lat":58.6,"lng":14.8,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":-39.21,"plng":-49.5},{"name":"First coral and stromatoporoid reefs","ma":465,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🪸","blurb":"Tabulate and rugose corals join sponge-like stromatoporoids to build the first true animal reefs, transforming the architecture of the seafloor.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Gondwana drifts over the South Pole","ma":450,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"🧊","blurb":"The vast southern supercontinent Gondwana centers on the South Pole, setting the stage for the ice caps that would end the Ordovician.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Hirnantian glaciation","ma":445,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🥶","blurb":"Massive ice sheets spread across polar Gondwana (today's Sahara), dropping sea levels dramatically and draining the shallow seas that teemed with life.","importance":5,"lat":25,"lng":10,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":-74.11,"plng":108.98},{"name":"First vascular land plants (Cooksonia)","ma":430,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🌱","blurb":"Cooksonia, a leafless dichotomously branching stem tipped with spore capsules, is among the earliest vascular plants, pioneering the plumbing (xylem) that let life stand upright away from water. Its tiny forked sprigs mark the true beginning of green land.","importance":5,"lat":52,"lng":-2.6,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-18.78,"plng":-71.76},{"name":"Age of Fishes: jawed vertebrates diversify","ma":420,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🐟","blurb":"The Devonian seas teemed with newly diversified jawed fish, armored placoderms, spiny acanthodians, early sharks and bony fish, in an evolutionary arms race of predator and prey. Jaws transformed vertebrates from filter-feeders into hunters.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Prototaxites giant fungal towers","ma":410,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🍄","blurb":"For millions of years the tallest organisms on land were Prototaxites, spire-like columns up to 8 m high and a metre wide, most likely enormous fungi that towered over knee-high plants. Nothing quite like them exists today.","importance":4,"lat":45.5,"lng":-64.3,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-35.74,"plng":-56.4},{"name":"First insects and land arthropods","ma":410,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🕷️","blurb":"Springtails, mites, the oldest known insect Rhyniognatha, early millipedes and trigonotarbid arachnids scuttled through Silurian-Devonian leaf litter, the first animals to make a permanent living on land. They built the earliest terrestrial food webs.","importance":4,"lat":57.4,"lng":-2.6,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-20.28,"plng":-40.95},{"name":"Oldest known coelacanth","ma":410,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🎣","blurb":"Lobe-finned coelacanths first appear in the Early Devonian, a lineage long thought extinct until a living one was hauled up off South Africa in 1938. They are among the most famous of 'living fossils'.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Ammonoids appear","ma":409,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🐚","blurb":"Coiled, chambered ammonoid cephalopods evolved from straighter nautiloid ancestors in the Early Devonian, beginning a 350-million-year dynasty of fast-evolving shells that make them prized index fossils. Their intricate sutures would grow ever more elaborate.","importance":3,"lat":49.9,"lng":6.5,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-21.64,"plng":-31.18},{"name":"Rhynie Chert ecosystem preserved","ma":407,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🔬","blurb":"A hot spring in what is now Aberdeenshire, Scotland flooded an early wetland with silica, entombing whole plants, fungi, mites, springtails and the oldest known lichens in exquisite cellular detail. It is the Pompeii of early terrestrial life.","importance":5,"lat":57.4,"lng":-2.6,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-20.28,"plng":-40.95},{"name":"First forests and trees (Archaeopteris)","ma":385,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🌲","blurb":"Archaeopteris, a fern-leaved tree with a true woody trunk up to 30 m tall, formed Earth's first real forests, sinking deep roots that weathered rock and drew down atmospheric CO2. Its spread reshaped soils, rivers and climate worldwide.","importance":5,"lat":42.6,"lng":-74.1,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-31.35,"plng":-58.38},{"name":"Tiktaalik: the fish that could do a push-up","ma":375,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🐊","blurb":"Discovered in 375-million-year-old Arctic Canadian rock, Tiktaalik had a flat crocodile-like head, a mobile neck, lungs and stout fin-limbs with wrist bones, a snapshot of vertebrates preparing to leave the water. It is the textbook fishapod transitional form.","importance":5,"lat":76,"lng":-88,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-0.28,"plng":-46.14},{"name":"Kellwasser event (Frasnian-Famennian extinction)","ma":372,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🌊","blurb":"Widespread ocean anoxia laid down two black shale layers and devastated the reef-building corals and stromatoporoids, one pulse of the drawn-out Late Devonian mass extinction. Tropical marine ecosystems collapsed and reefs would not fully recover for over 100 million years.","importance":4,"lat":50.7,"lng":6.9,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-21.59,"plng":-17.69},{"name":"Tetrapods walk onto land (Greenland)","ma":365,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🦎","blurb":"Ichthyostega and Acanthostega, four-limbed vertebrates from the swamps of East Greenland, had digits (up to eight per limb) yet still breathed partly through gills. Their fossils capture the moment fins became feet.","importance":5,"lat":72,"lng":-24,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-9.15,"plng":-22.94},{"name":"Hangenberg event (end-Devonian extinction)","ma":359,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"❄️","blurb":"A final anoxic and cooling crisis at the Devonian's close wiped out the armored placoderms entirely and gutted early vertebrate diversity, followed by a survival bottleneck called Romer's Gap. It reset the cast for the coming Age of Amphibians.","importance":4,"lat":50.7,"lng":6.9,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-20.28,"plng":-12.47},{"name":"Late Paleozoic Ice Age","ma":330,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🧊","blurb":"With Gondwana over the South Pole and CO2 drawn down by the coal forests, huge ice sheets spread across what are now South Africa, India, Australia and Antarctica, driving repeated glacial-interglacial cycles that shaped coal cyclothems.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Assembly of Pangaea","ma":320,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"🌍","blurb":"The collision of Gondwana with Euramerica (the Variscan/Hercynian orogeny) welded nearly all continental crust into the single supercontinent Pangaea, surrounded by the world-ocean Panthalassa. The final suturing continued through the Permian as Siberia and Kazakhstania docked.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Peak Coal Forests","ma":315,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🌳","blurb":"Vast equatorial swamp forests of giant lycopsids (Lepidodendron, Sigillaria), horsetails (Calamites) and ferns buried so much undecayed carbon that they formed most of the world's coal seams and drew CO2 down sharply. The very word Carboniferous means 'coal-bearing'.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Origin of the amniotic egg","ma":315,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🥚","blurb":"The evolution of a shelled egg with protective membranes let vertebrates reproduce entirely on dry land for the first time, freeing tetrapods from the water's edge and founding the two great amniote lineages, reptiles and synapsids.","importance":5,"lat":45.9,"lng":-64.6,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":-5.56,"plng":-3.77},{"name":"Ouachita orogeny","ma":310,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"⛰️","blurb":"As Gondwana ground into southern Laurussia, the Ouachita-Marathon mountain belt buckled up across what is now the southern United States, part of the same collision that raised the Appalachians and assembled Pangaea.","importance":2,"lat":34.6,"lng":-94.4,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":-3.68,"plng":-28.13},{"name":"Carboniferous Rainforest Collapse","ma":305,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🍂","blurb":"A pulse of drying and warming fragmented the once-continuous coal swamps into isolated patches, collapsing the lycopsid rainforests and giving tree ferns and, later, seed plants the advantage; it also fragmented amphibian habitats and favored the newly evolved amniotes.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Oxygen peak (~35% O2)","ma":300,"kind":"atmosphere","emoji":"💨","blurb":"Prolific burial of forest carbon pushed atmospheric oxygen to roughly 30-35%, the highest in Earth's history, thin air rich enough to let insects and other arthropods breathing through passive tracheae grow to gigantic sizes.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Rise of conifers and glossopterids","ma":290,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🌲","blurb":"As Pangaea's interior dried, seed plants took over: true conifers, cycads and ginkgos spread through the north while the seed fern Glossopteris blanketed cool, wet Gondwana, its distribution later becoming key evidence for continental drift.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Zechstein Sea evaporites","ma":257,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🧂","blurb":"A shallow, repeatedly evaporating sea across northern Pangaea (today's North Sea and Germany) laid down thick beds of salt and potash, a signature of the hot, arid Permian supercontinent interior.","importance":2,"lat":52,"lng":10,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":20.1,"plng":29.51},{"name":"Siberian Traps eruptions","ma":251.9,"kind":"volcanic","emoji":"🌋","blurb":"One of the largest volcanic events ever known flooded millions of square kilometers of Siberia with basalt and, crucially, cooked coal and carbonate beds, belching CO2, methane and toxic gases that drove the end-Permian mass extinction.","importance":5,"lat":67,"lng":90,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":63.48,"plng":39.74},{"name":"Ocean anoxia and acidification","ma":251.9,"kind":"ocean","emoji":"🌊","blurb":"Runaway greenhouse warming from the Siberian Traps stripped oxygen from the seas and acidified surface waters, suffocating and dissolving marine life during the Great Dying and leaving a global carbon-isotope collapse in the rocks.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Permian-Triassic boundary","ma":251.9,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"📉","blurb":"The GSSP at Meishan in China marks Earth's most severe biotic crisis, a thin clay bed above which fusulinids, trilobites, rugose corals and most of the Paleozoic fauna simply vanish, ending the Paleozoic Era.","importance":5,"lat":31.1,"lng":119.7,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":19.04,"plng":101.82},{"name":"Pangaea at maximum assembly","ma":250,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"🌍","blurb":"The supercontinent Pangaea reached its greatest coherence, a single C-shaped landmass surrounded by the world-ocean Panthalassa, with the Tethys Sea biting into its eastern flank. Its vast interior became a searingly arid, seasonally extreme desert far from any coast.","lat":null,"lng":null,"importance":5,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Siberian Traps aftermath and slow biotic recovery","ma":250,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🥵","blurb":"In the earliest Triassic, greenhouse heat lingering from the end-Permian Siberian Traps kept equatorial seas lethally hot (near 40 C), and full ecosystem recovery took roughly 5 million years. 'Disaster taxa' like the clam Claraia and the coal-gap fern spore Lystrosaurus flora briefly dominated barren landscapes.","lat":null,"lng":null,"importance":4,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Carnian Pluvial Episode","ma":233,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🌧️","blurb":"Around 233 Ma a roughly one-to-two-million-year burst of humid, rainy climate interrupted Pangaea's arid regime, likely triggered by Wrangellia volcanism. This wet pulse coincides with the diversification of dinosaurs, the first conifers of modern type, and a major turnover in marine and terrestrial life.","lat":null,"lng":null,"importance":4,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"First dinosaurs appear","ma":233,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🦖","blurb":"The oldest well-dated dinosaurs, small bipedal predators like Eoraptor and Herrerasaurus, appear in the Ischigualasto Formation of Argentina around 233–231 Ma. They were minor players sharing the land with larger crocodile-line archosaurs and mammal ancestors.","lat":-30.16,"lng":-67.99,"importance":5,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":-42.98,"plng":-30.96},{"name":"Origin of pterosaurs (first flying vertebrates)","ma":228,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🪽","blurb":"By the Late Triassic, pterosaurs such as Eudimorphodon had evolved powered flight on a wing membrane stretched from an enormously elongated fourth finger. They were the first vertebrates to conquer the air, tens of millions of years before birds.","lat":null,"lng":null,"importance":5,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Origin of the first true mammals","ma":210,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🐭","blurb":"Tiny nocturnal mammaliaforms like Morganucodon, no bigger than a shrew, arose from cynodont ancestors in the Late Triassic. They had a novel jaw joint, differentiated teeth, and likely warm blood and fur, ushering in over 200 million years of mammalian evolution in the shadow of dinosaurs.","lat":null,"lng":null,"importance":5,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Central Atlantic Magmatic Province (CAMP) eruptions","ma":201.5,"kind":"volcanic","emoji":"🌋","blurb":"One of Earth's largest known volcanic events flooded the rifting heart of Pangaea with basalt across what is now the Atlantic margins of the Americas, Africa, and Europe. Its CO2 and sulfur triggered the end-Triassic mass extinction and helped tear Pangaea open.","lat":null,"lng":null,"importance":5,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Pangaea begins to rift apart","ma":195,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"🪓","blurb":"As the Jurassic opened, the central Atlantic began to widen and Pangaea split into northern Laurasia and southern Gondwana. Rifting flooded continental interiors with new seaways, spreading warm, humid climates and shallow marine habitats.","lat":null,"lng":null,"importance":4,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Break-up widens Tethys and central Atlantic","ma":165,"kind":"ocean","emoji":"🌊","blurb":"By the Middle-Late Jurassic, seafloor spreading had opened a true, if narrow, central Atlantic Ocean and broadened the Tethys, connecting warm marine faunas across the globe. Reefs, ammonites, and marine reptiles spread through these expanding tropical seaways.","lat":null,"lng":null,"importance":3,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Sundance and Morrison ecosystems flourish","ma":155,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🏜️","blurb":"The Late Jurassic Morrison Formation of the western United States preserves a vast semi-arid floodplain teeming with giant sauropods, Stegosaurus, and Allosaurus. It is the richest single window into the classic 'Age of Giants' dinosaur world.","lat":40.2,"lng":-108,"importance":4,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":30.48,"plng":-36.84},{"name":"First birds evolve from theropod dinosaurs","ma":150,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🐦","blurb":"Archaeopteryx, preserved in the fine limestone of Solnhofen, Germany, shows feathered wings alongside teeth, clawed fingers, and a long bony tail. It captures the transition from small feathered dinosaurs to true flying birds.","lat":48.9,"lng":11.2,"importance":5,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":31.09,"plng":37.9},{"name":"Solnhofen lagoon Lagerstätte deposited","ma":150,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🪨","blurb":"Stagnant, oxygen-poor tropical lagoons in what is now Bavaria buried delicate organisms in exquisitely fine lime mud. The result is a snapshot of a Jurassic archipelago preserving feathers, jellyfish, insects, and the first bird in astonishing detail.","lat":48.9,"lng":11.2,"importance":3,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":31.09,"plng":37.9},{"name":"Cretaceous Period begins","ma":143.1,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🌍","blurb":"The final period of the Mesozoic opens on a hothouse world with no polar ice caps, sea levels far above today's, and a widening Atlantic slicing between the Americas and Europe-Africa.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Rise of the flowering plants (angiosperm radiation)","ma":125,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🌸","blurb":"Angiosperms burst onto the scene and diversify explosively through the mid-Cretaceous, out-competing ferns and conifers in many habitats — Darwin called their sudden appearance an 'abominable mystery'.","importance":5,"lat":41,"lng":123,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":48.24,"plng":144.19},{"name":"South America and Africa split (South Atlantic opens)","ma":110,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"🌊","blurb":"The last land connection between South America and Africa tears apart as the South Atlantic rift propagates northward, fully separating the two continents and isolating their faunas.","importance":5,"lat":-5,"lng":0,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":-5,"plng":0},{"name":"Western Interior Seaway floods North America","ma":95,"kind":"ocean","emoji":"🐚","blurb":"A shallow tropical sea splits North America in two from the Arctic to the Gulf of Mexico, teeming with ammonites, giant fish, plesiosaurs and mosasaurs.","importance":4,"lat":40,"lng":-105,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":44.31,"plng":-57.07},{"name":"Cenomanian-Turonian Ocean Anoxic Event (OAE 2)","ma":94,"kind":"ocean","emoji":"🖤","blurb":"A worldwide burst of ocean anoxia buried vast amounts of black organic-rich shale, drawing down carbon and driving a significant marine extinction pulse.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Cretaceous Thermal Maximum","ma":92,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🔥","blurb":"Global temperatures peak in the Turonian, with tropical sea-surface waters exceeding 35°C and crocodile relatives and lush forests reaching high latitudes near both poles.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"India rifts from Madagascar and races north","ma":88,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"🧭","blurb":"The Indian subcontinent breaks free of Madagascar and begins a fast solo drift northward across the Tethys Ocean, on a collision course with Asia tens of millions of years in the future.","importance":4,"lat":-20,"lng":45,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":-39.74,"plng":41.26},{"name":"Deccan Traps flood-basalt eruptions begin","ma":66.3,"kind":"volcanic","emoji":"🌋","blurb":"One of Earth's largest volcanic provinces erupts across western India, spewing hundreds of thousands of cubic kilometers of lava and pumping climate-altering gases into the atmosphere around the time of the K-Pg crisis.","importance":5,"lat":18.5,"lng":73.8,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":-31.02,"plng":58.73},{"name":"Chicxulub asteroid impact","ma":66,"kind":"impact","emoji":"☄️","blurb":"A roughly 10-km asteroid slams into the Yucatán, gouging a 180-km crater, triggering global wildfires, tsunamis, and an impact winter that blots out the sun — the abrupt trigger of the K-Pg mass extinction.","importance":5,"lat":21.4,"lng":-89.5,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":25.48,"plng":-60.39},{"name":"Iridium anomaly layer laid down","ma":66,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"📏","blurb":"A thin global clay layer enriched in the rare element iridium marks the exact K-Pg boundary worldwide, the smoking-gun evidence for an extraterrestrial impact first found near Gubbio, Italy.","importance":4,"lat":43.35,"lng":12.58,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":27.19,"plng":20.46},{"name":"Deccan Traps volcanism (waning)","ma":66,"kind":"volcanic","emoji":"🌋","blurb":"Vast flood basalt eruptions in India, which had straddled the K-Pg boundary, continued into the earliest Paleocene, venting climate-altering gases. Their eruptions overlap the dinosaur extinction and the dawn of the mammal age.","importance":3,"lat":18.5,"lng":73.8,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":-31.02,"plng":58.73},{"name":"Paleocene-Eocene Thermal Maximum (PETM)","ma":56,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🌡️","blurb":"A massive release of carbon dioxide and methane spiked global temperatures by 5-8°C in a few thousand years, acidifying oceans and driving a burst of mammal dispersal across Northern Hemisphere land bridges. It is the best geologic analog for rapid modern warming.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"India collides with Asia, Himalaya rise","ma":50,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"⛰️","blurb":"The northward-drifting Indian plate slammed into Eurasia, crumpling the crust upward to raise the Himalaya and the Tibetan Plateau. The growing mountains reshaped monsoon circulation and drew down atmospheric CO2 over tens of millions of years.","importance":5,"lat":27.99,"lng":86.93,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":1.89,"plng":81.15},{"name":"Chesapeake Bay bolide impact","ma":35,"kind":"impact","emoji":"☄️","blurb":"A large asteroid or comet struck the shallow sea over what is now the U.S. mid-Atlantic coast, blasting an 85-km crater buried beneath Chesapeake Bay. It is one of the best-preserved late Eocene impact structures.","importance":2,"lat":37.28,"lng":-76.02,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":33.54,"plng":-55.51},{"name":"Eocene-Oligocene transition & Antarctic glaciation","ma":34,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🧊","blurb":"Cooling CO2 and the opening of Southern Ocean gateways let permanent ice sheets form on Antarctica for the first time, plunging Earth from a hothouse into an icehouse. The 'Grande Coupure' turnover swept away many archaic mammal groups.","importance":5,"lat":-75,"lng":0,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":-79.73,"plng":8.77},{"name":"Drake Passage opens, Antarctic Circumpolar Current","ma":34,"kind":"ocean","emoji":"🌊","blurb":"As South America finally separated from Antarctica, a continuous circumpolar current locked cold water around the pole and thermally isolated the continent. This ocean circulation change helped trigger and sustain Antarctic ice.","importance":4,"lat":-58,"lng":-63,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":-58,"plng":-63},{"name":"Grasslands and savannas expand","ma":25,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🌾","blurb":"Cooler, drier, more seasonal climates let grasses spread into vast open plains, favoring fast-running, high-crowned-toothed grazers like horses and antelope. Grass-fueled ecosystems reshaped mammalian evolution worldwide.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Mid-Miocene Climatic Optimum","ma":15.5,"kind":"climate","emoji":"☀️","blurb":"A ~2 million-year warm spell pushed temperatures several degrees above today's, expanding forests and boosting diversity before a renewed slide into cooling. Apes flourished and spread across Africa and Eurasia during this interval.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Messinian Salinity Crisis","ma":5.96,"kind":"geologic","emoji":"🧂","blurb":"The Mediterranean was cut off from the Atlantic and largely dried into a giant salt basin, leaving kilometer-thick evaporite deposits. When the Strait of Gibraltar breached ~5.33 Ma, a cataclysmic flood refilled the sea.","importance":4,"lat":36,"lng":-5.35,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":31.21,"plng":-4.94},{"name":"Isthmus of Panama closes","ma":3,"kind":"tectonic","emoji":"🌎","blurb":"The rising Central American land bridge joined North and South America and severed the Atlantic from the Pacific, triggering the Great American Biotic Interchange. It rerouted ocean currents and helped intensify Northern Hemisphere glaciation.","importance":5,"lat":9,"lng":-79.5,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":9.0,"plng":-79.5},{"name":"Great American Biotic Interchange","ma":3,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🦥","blurb":"Across the new Panama land bridge, North American placentals (cats, canids, horses) poured south while giant ground sloths, armadillos, and opossums moved north. Many endemic South American mammals were later outcompeted to extinction.","importance":4,"lat":9,"lng":-79.5,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":9.0,"plng":-79.5},{"name":"Onset of Northern Hemisphere glaciation","ma":2.7,"kind":"climate","emoji":"❄️","blurb":"Large ice sheets began cyclically advancing over North America and Eurasia, marking the deepening of the ice ages that define the coming Quaternary. Cold, dry, variable climates spurred grassland spread and megafaunal evolution.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Onset of Quaternary glaciations (Pleistocene begins)","ma":2.58,"kind":"climate","emoji":"❄️","blurb":"The Quaternary opens as Northern Hemisphere ice sheets begin their rhythmic advance and retreat, driven by cyclical wobbles in Earth's orbit. This icehouse world would repeatedly bury much of Europe and North America under kilometres of ice.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Middle Pleistocene Transition (100 kyr cycles)","ma":0.9,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🔄","blurb":"Glacial cycles switched from a gentle 41,000-year beat to punishing 100,000-year swings, producing the deepest ice ages with vast continental sheets. The cause of this shift remains one of climate science's great puzzles.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Toba supervolcanic eruption","ma":0.074,"kind":"volcanic","emoji":"🌋","blurb":"Sumatra's Lake Toba unleashed the largest eruption of the last two million years, blasting out roughly 2,800 cubic kilometres of magma and dusting South Asia in ash. It may briefly have chilled the planet and squeezed early human populations.","importance":4,"lat":2.6,"lng":98.8,"era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":2.6,"plng":98.8},{"name":"Last Glacial Maximum","ma":0.021,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🧊","blurb":"Ice reached its greatest extent, locking up so much water that sea level fell about 120 metres and exposed land bridges like Beringia and Doggerland. Mammoths roamed a cold, dry 'steppe-tundra' spanning three continents.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"First humans reach the Americas","ma":0.015,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"👣","blurb":"People crossed the Bering land bridge and spread down through the Americas, the last habitable continents to be settled. Fossil footprints at White Sands hint humans may have arrived even earlier, over 20,000 years ago.","importance":5,"lat":32.8,"lng":-106.3,"era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":32.8,"plng":-106.3},{"name":"Younger Dryas cold snap","ma":0.0128,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🥶","blurb":"A sudden 1,200-year plunge back into near-glacial cold interrupted the warming, likely when meltwater floods disrupted Atlantic ocean currents. Its abrupt end warmed Greenland by up to 10 degrees within decades.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Holocene begins (current interglacial)","ma":0.0117,"kind":"climate","emoji":"🌅","blurb":"The ice retreated into a warm, stable interglacial that has lasted ever since, the climatic stage on which all of human civilization was built. Its formal boundary is pinned to a layer in a Greenland ice core.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Birth of agriculture (Neolithic Revolution)","ma":0.0115,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🌾","blurb":"In the Fertile Crescent people began domesticating wheat, barley, sheep and goats, trading foraging for farming. Settled villages, surplus food and eventually cities followed within a few thousand years.","importance":5,"lat":37,"lng":38.9,"era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":37.0,"plng":38.9},{"name":"Proposed Anthropocene onset (Great Acceleration)","ma":7e-05,"kind":"milestone","emoji":"🏭","blurb":"Around 1950 humanity's fingerprint on the planet became a geological signal: fossil-carbon isotopes, plastics, concrete and radioactive fallout from nuclear tests now mark the sediments. Many scientists argue this defines a new human-dominated epoch.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":null,"plng":null}];

const LIFE = [{"name":"Prebiotic organic chemistry","appear_ma":4200,"clade":"prebiotic","common":"Building blocks of life","emoji":"🧪","blurb":"Amino acids, nucleobases, and other carbon compounds accumulated on the young Earth, delivered by meteorites and forged at volcanic vents and in tidal pools. These molecules were the raw ingredients from which life would later self-assemble, though no living thing yet existed.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Hadean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"LUCA (Last Universal Common Ancestor)","appear_ma":4200,"clade":"prebiotic","emoji":"🌱","blurb":"The single ancestral cell population from which all bacteria, archaea and eukaryotes descend, already equipped with DNA, ribosomes, a genetic code and ATP synthase. Reconstructions suggest it was an anaerobic, hydrogen-dependent thermophile living off simple gases.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":null,"common":"Last universal common ancestor","lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"EoArchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Possible earliest life (contested ≥4.0 Ga)","appear_ma":4000,"clade":"prebiotic","common":"First hints of life","emoji":"🤠","blurb":"Controversial carbon-isotope signatures in ~4.1-billion-year-old Jack Hills zircons and graphite have been read by some as the faintest chemical whisper of life, though most researchers regard the evidence as inconclusive. If real, it would push life's origin to the very edge of the Hadean.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":-26.1,"lng":116.9,"era":"Hadean","plat":-26.1,"plng":116.9},{"name":"Iron-oxidizing microbes (BIF contributors)","appear_ma":3800,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🧲","blurb":"Microbes that gained energy by oxidizing dissolved iron may have helped precipitate the banded iron formations of Isua. Their metabolism turned the iron-rich Archean sea into the rusty layered rocks we mine today.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":null,"common":"Iron-oxidizing microbes","lat":65.1,"lng":-50,"era":"EoArchean","plat":65.1,"plng":-50},{"name":"Nuvvuagittuq tube-and-filament microfossils","appear_ma":3800,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🧵","blurb":"Hematite tubes and filaments in Quebec's Nuvvuagittuq banded iron formation resemble structures made by modern vent-dwelling iron bacteria. If biological, they would be among the oldest putative body fossils of life on Earth.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":3800,"common":"Putative vent microfossils","lat":58.3,"lng":-77.7,"era":"EoArchean","plat":58.3,"plng":-77.7},{"name":"Earliest bacteria (domain Bacteria)","appear_ma":3700,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🦠","blurb":"One of the two great prokaryotic domains, single cells without a nucleus that quickly diversified into the metabolisms running Earth's chemistry. Their descendants still dominate the biosphere by sheer number and biomass.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":null,"common":"Bacteria","lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"EoArchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Earliest archaea (domain Archaea)","appear_ma":3700,"clade":"archaea","emoji":"🧫","blurb":"The second prokaryotic domain, superficially bacteria-like but biochemically distinct, with unique ether-linked membrane lipids that thrive in extreme heat, acidity and salt. From within this domain the eukaryotic lineage would eventually emerge.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":null,"common":"Archaea","lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"EoArchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Chemolithotrophic prokaryotes","appear_ma":3700,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🔩","blurb":"Microbes that harvest energy directly from inorganic chemistry, oxidizing hydrogen, sulfur or iron rather than eating other organisms or basking in sunlight. In a lightless vent world, rock and water were the whole menu.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":null,"common":"Rock-eating microbes","lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"EoArchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Methanogens","appear_ma":3500,"clade":"archaea","emoji":"💨","blurb":"Archaea that make a living by combining hydrogen and carbon dioxide to produce methane, one of the most ancient metabolisms known. Their exhaust helped build the methane greenhouse that kept the young, faint-Sun Earth warm.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":null,"common":"Methane-making microbes","lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"EoArchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Anoxygenic phototrophs","appear_ma":3500,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🌅","blurb":"Early photosynthesizers that captured sunlight using hydrogen sulfide or ferrous iron as an electron source and released no oxygen at all. This 'old' style of photosynthesis long predated the oxygen-making kind that would later transform the planet.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":null,"common":"Non-oxygen photosynthesizers","lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"EoArchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Acetogens","appear_ma":3500,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🧪","blurb":"Bacteria that fix carbon dioxide into acetate using the ancient Wood-Ljungdahl pathway, a metabolic route so simple and vent-compatible that it may echo the very chemistry of life's origin. Like methanogens, they thrive without oxygen or sunlight.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":null,"common":"Acetate-making microbes","lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"EoArchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Obligate anaerobes (retreating)","appear_ma":3500,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🚫","blurb":"To the ancient microbes that had ruled the anoxic Earth, oxygen was a corrosive poison, and the GOE drove them into muds, deep waters, and hot springs. Their descendants still hide in these oxygen-free refugia billions of years later.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Stromatolite-building microbial mats","appear_ma":3480,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🥞","blurb":"Sticky films of prokaryotes that trapped and bound sediment grain by grain, growing into the layered domes preserved at Strelley Pool. They are the oldest structures widely accepted as physical evidence of living communities.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":null,"common":"Mat-building microbes","lat":-21.15,"lng":119.4,"era":"EoArchean","plat":-21.15,"plng":119.4},{"name":"Sulfate-reducing bacteria","appear_ma":3470,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🟡","blurb":"Anaerobes that breathe sulfate instead of oxygen, leaving telltale sulfur-isotope patterns in Pilbara barites dated to about 3.47 billion years. They rank among the earliest metabolisms for which we have direct chemical evidence.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":null,"common":"Sulfate breathers","lat":-21.1,"lng":119.4,"era":"EoArchean","plat":-21.1,"plng":119.4},{"name":"Apex chert filamentous microstructures","appear_ma":3465,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🔬","blurb":"Carbon-walled filaments in the 3.46-billion-year-old Apex chert of the Pilbara were long championed as the oldest cellular microfossils, though their biological origin has been fiercely debated. Recent carbon-isotope analyses of individual filaments revived the case that at least some are genuine cells.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":3465,"common":"Apex chert microfossils","lat":-21.5,"lng":119.4,"era":"EoArchean","plat":-21.5,"plng":119.4},{"name":"Purple and green anoxygenic phototrophs","appear_ma":3400,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🟣","blurb":"These photosynthesizers use light without releasing oxygen, drawing on hydrogen, sulfide, or iron instead, and persisted in the stratified oceans below the oxygenated surface. They represent photosynthesis's older, oxygen-free heritage.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Anoxygenic photosynthetic bacteria","appear_ma":3200,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🟣","blurb":"Purple and green bacteria that ran an older style of photosynthesis using hydrogen, sulfur, or iron instead of water, producing no oxygen. They thrived in the sunlit but oxygen-free Archean shallows.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":null,"lng":null,"common":"Purple & green bacteria","era":"Neoarchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Methanogenic archaea","appear_ma":3200,"clade":"archaea","emoji":"💨","blurb":"Ancient archaea that made a living producing methane from carbon dioxide and hydrogen. Their methane is thought to have warmed the early Earth under a faint young Sun, keeping the oceans liquid.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":null,"lng":null,"common":"Methanogens","era":"Neoarchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Nitrogen-fixing microbes","appear_ma":3200,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🌫️","blurb":"Microbes able to pull inert nitrogen gas from the air and turn it into biologically usable ammonia, an essential step for feeding an expanding biosphere. Nitrogen isotopes suggest biological nitrogen fixation was active deep in the Archean.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":null,"lng":null,"common":"Nitrogen fixers","era":"Neoarchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Filamentous & coccoidal microfossils","appear_ma":3000,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🔬","blurb":"Microscopic thread-like and spherical cell remains preserved in Archean cherts document a genuinely diverse prokaryote world. Their shapes hint at communities as varied as those in modern microbial mats.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":-21,"lng":119.5,"common":"Microfossils","era":"Neoarchean","plat":-21,"plng":119.5},{"name":"Iron-oxidizing bacteria","appear_ma":3000,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🧲","blurb":"Microbes that gain energy by oxidizing dissolved iron likely helped precipitate some of the era's colossal banded iron formations. They flourished at the chemical frontier where oxygen-poor and oxygen-rich waters met.","importance":3,"lat":-22.3,"lng":118.5,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian","plat":-22.3,"plng":118.5},{"name":"Cyanobacteria (oxygenic phototrophs)","appear_ma":2900,"clade":"cyanobacteria","emoji":"🦠","blurb":"The great oxygen-makers: microbes that harvested sunlight by splitting water, releasing O2 and building layered stromatolite mats. Their slow, relentless output would eventually oxygenate the entire planet.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":-21,"lng":119.5,"common":"Cyanobacteria","era":"Neoarchean","plat":-21,"plng":119.5},{"name":"Methanotrophic microbes","appear_ma":2800,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🔥","blurb":"Microbes that consumed methane, recorded by extremely light carbon isotopes in 2.7-billion-year-old shales such as the Fortescue Group. Their signatures show an active Archean methane cycle.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":-22.3,"lng":119.7,"common":"Methane eaters","era":"Neoarchean","plat":-22.3,"plng":119.7},{"name":"Iron-oxidising bacteria","appear_ma":2800,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🟥","blurb":"Microbes that gained energy by oxidising dissolved ferrous iron, possibly contributing to the precipitation of banded iron formations. They flourished at the boundary between iron-rich water and scarce oxidants.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":null,"lng":null,"common":"Iron oxidisers","era":"Neoarchean","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Oxygenic cyanobacteria","appear_ma":2700,"clade":"cyanobacteria","emoji":"🦠","blurb":"By the early Paleoproterozoic these photosynthesizers were pumping out oxygen fast enough to overwhelm the planet's chemical sinks and transform the atmosphere. They are the engineers of the Great Oxidation Event and still fringe every shoreline and pond today.","importance":5,"lat":-22.3,"lng":118.5,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian","plat":-22.3,"plng":118.5},{"name":"Aerobic (oxygen-respiring) bacteria","appear_ma":2400,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"💠","blurb":"As free oxygen spread, lineages that could harness it for respiration gained an enormous energy advantage over their fermenting neighbors. Aerobic metabolism, once a curiosity, became the dominant way of making a living in the light zone.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Early eukaryotes (stem lineage)","appear_ma":2400,"clade":"eukaryote","emoji":"🧫","blurb":"Oxygen-rich waters set the stage for cells with a nucleus and mitochondria, which arose when a host cell engulfed an aerobic bacterium. Molecular clocks place the eukaryote stem in this window, though undisputed body fossils come later.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Diskagma buttonii","appear_ma":2200,"clade":"eukaryote","emoji":"🫙","blurb":"Tiny urn-shaped structures from an ancient South African soil hint that eukaryote-grade organisms may have ventured onto land surfaces astonishingly early, though their exact nature is debated. They are among the most enigmatic fossils of the age.","importance":2,"lat":-27.7,"lng":30.4,"extinct_ma":2200,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":-27.7,"plng":30.4},{"name":"Francevillian biota (Gabon macrofossils)","appear_ma":2100,"clade":"eukaryote","emoji":"🌀","blurb":"Centimeter-scale disc- and lobe-shaped fossils from black shales in Gabon may be the oldest large, possibly multicellular organisms, appearing right after the oxygen pulse. Their true biological affinity remains fiercely debated but tantalizing.","importance":4,"lat":-1.5,"lng":13,"extinct_ma":2020,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian","plat":-1.5,"plng":13},{"name":"Grypania-like coiled microfossils","appear_ma":2100,"clade":"eukaryote","emoji":"➰","blurb":"Millimeter-wide spiral carbonaceous ribbons found in Paleoproterozoic rocks are candidates for early eukaryotic algae, hinting at organized, coiled body plans. Whether truly eukaryotic or giant bacterial colonies is still argued.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Stromatolite-building cyanobacteria","appear_ma":2050,"clade":"cyanobacteria","emoji":"🪨","blurb":"Oxygen-releasing cyanobacteria kept constructing layered mineral mounds called stromatolites in shallow seas worldwide, sustaining the slow oxygenation of the planet. Their photosynthesis set the chemical stage for eukaryotic life.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Sulphate-reducing bacteria","appear_ma":2050,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🟡","blurb":"As oxygen weathered sulphur into seawater, sulphate-reducing microbes flourished and helped drive the oceans toward sulphide-rich conditions in the mid-Proterozoic. Their metabolism reshaped the chemistry of the deep sea.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Asgard-related host archaeon","appear_ma":2000,"clade":"archaea","emoji":"🦠","blurb":"The eukaryotic cell's host body traces back to an Asgard-type archaeon, a microbe whose modern relatives grow long branching protrusions that could have first entangled the bacterium destined to become the mitochondrion. It supplied the nucleus and cytoskeleton of complex life.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Grypania spiralis","appear_ma":1900,"clade":"eukaryote","emoji":"🌀","blurb":"Coiled, ribbon-like carbon films up to several centimetres long, preserved in Michigan iron formations, are among the oldest fossils large enough to see with the naked eye. Many researchers interpret them as a giant coenocytic alga, one of the earliest candidate eukaryotes.","importance":5,"lat":46.4,"lng":-87.6,"extinct_ma":1600,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":46.4,"plng":-87.6},{"name":"Gunflint microbiota","appear_ma":1880,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🧫","blurb":"A diverse community of filamentous and spherical microbes, including the iconic umbrella-shaped Kakabekia, silicified in the Gunflint chert. Many were iron- and oxygen-cycling bacteria thriving around a shallow sea.","importance":4,"lat":48.4,"lng":-89.6,"extinct_ma":1600,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":48.4,"plng":-89.6},{"name":"Gunflintia (filamentous microfossil)","appear_ma":1880,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🧵","blurb":"Slender tubular filaments, probably iron-oxidising or cyanobacterial sheaths, are the most abundant fossils of the Gunflint chert. They record microbial mats that helped precipitate the last great banded iron formations.","importance":3,"lat":48.4,"lng":-89.6,"extinct_ma":1600,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":48.4,"plng":-89.6},{"name":"Acritarchs (organic-walled microfossils)","appear_ma":1800,"clade":"protist","emoji":"⚪","blurb":"Hollow, tough-walled microscopic spheres, often spiny, drifted through Proterozoic seas and are widely regarded as cysts of early planktonic eukaryotes. They become Earth's first rich fossil record of complex-celled plankton.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Mitochondrion (endosymbiotic origin)","appear_ma":1700,"clade":"eukaryote","emoji":"🔋","blurb":"An engulfed alphaproteobacterium was not digested but domesticated, becoming the mitochondrion, the powerhouse that let the eukaryotic cell burn oxygen efficiently. This single symbiotic merger underlies all complex life, from mushrooms to whales.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Last eukaryotic common ancestor (LECA)","appear_ma":1700,"clade":"eukaryote","emoji":"🧬","blurb":"The ancestor of every living eukaryote already possessed a nucleus, an internal skeleton, sexual reproduction and mitochondria, a cell of startling sophistication. All animals, plants, fungi and protists descend from this single lineage.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Valeria (ornamented acritarch)","appear_ma":1650,"clade":"protist","emoji":"🟠","blurb":"A large acritarch whose wall is patterned with a distinctive polygonal or scaly ornament, marking a jump in the structural complexity of Proterozoic plankton. Such ornament is hard for simple bacteria to build.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":1000,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Tappania","appear_ma":1600,"clade":"eukaryote","emoji":"🌵","blurb":"A microfossil with a flask-shaped body sprouting branching, budding tubular arms, showing a level of shape control that only a true eukaryotic cell could achieve. Found across India, Australia and China, it is a strong early eukaryote.","importance":4,"lat":24,"lng":82,"extinct_ma":1400,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":24,"plng":82},{"name":"Bangiomorpha-lineage red algae precursors","appear_ma":1600,"clade":"plant:algae","emoji":"🌿","blurb":"Toward the close of this interval the ancestors of the first multicellular red algae were emerging, cells beginning to divide labour into simple filaments. They foreshadow the eukaryotic photosynthesisers that would populate later seas.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Marine cyanobacteria","appear_ma":1600,"clade":"cyanobacteria","emoji":"🦠","blurb":"Oxygen-producing cyanobacteria form the base of Boring Billion ecosystems as mats, stromatolites, and plankton, sustaining the biosphere through the long chemical stasis. Their photosynthesis kept surface waters oxygenated even as the deep sea stayed anoxic.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Eukaryotic acritarchs","appear_ma":1600,"clade":"eukaryote","emoji":"🔮","blurb":"Organic-walled microfossils of uncertain affinity, mostly the resting cysts of planktonic protists, are the most abundant and widespread eukaryotic fossils of the era. Their spines and ornamentation slowly grow more elaborate through the Mesoproterozoic.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Benthic microbial mats and stromatolites","appear_ma":1600,"clade":"bacteria","emoji":"🟫","blurb":"Layered microbial communities carpet shallow seafloors and build laminated stromatolites, the dominant reef-builders of the age. Their diversity actually peaks and then gently declines across the Boring Billion.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Horodyskia (string-of-beads organism)","appear_ma":1500,"clade":"eukaryote","emoji":"📿","blurb":"A puzzling fossil preserved as rows of regularly spaced beads, possibly one of the earliest large multicellular or colonial organisms. Whether it was a giant protist, an alga, or something else entirely remains debated.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":900,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Bangiomorpha pubescens","appear_ma":1050,"clade":"plant:algae","emoji":"🔴","blurb":"A filamentous multicellular red alga from Arctic Canada with a holdfast anchoring it to the seafloor and cells specialized for producing spores, marking the oldest known sexually reproducing organism. Its bangiophyte body plan is essentially identical to a modern red seaweed.","importance":5,"lat":73,"lng":-80,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":73,"plng":-80},{"name":"Red algae (Rhodophyta)","appear_ma":1050,"clade":"plant:algae","emoji":"🌺","blurb":"The rhodophytes, born from the primary plastid endosymbiosis, are among the first lineages of complex multicellular photosynthesizers. Their descendants still redden reefs and shorelines a billion years later.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Multicellular eukaryotes (Archaeplastida)","appear_ma":1050,"clade":"eukaryote","emoji":"🧩","blurb":"The red and green algal lineages independently achieve true multicellularity with cell differentiation, the deepest roots of complex tissue-grade life. Sexual reproduction and multicellularity together vastly expanded the evolutionary possibilities of eukaryotes.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Green algae (Chlorophyta/Viridiplantae)","appear_ma":1000,"clade":"plant:algae","emoji":"🟢","blurb":"Early green algae diverge within the Archaeplastida, inheriting the chlorophyll-a/b plastid that would one day power all land plants. Molecular clocks and rare fossils place their origin deep in the late Mesoproterozoic.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Proterocladus antiquus","appear_ma":1000,"clade":"plant:algae","emoji":"🌿","blurb":"A millimeter-scale branching green seaweed from North China, among the oldest convincing multicellular green-algal fossils. It shows that chlorophytes had achieved differentiated, upright, branching bodies by the end of the Mesoproterozoic.","importance":4,"lat":39,"lng":117,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":35.57,"plng":160.79},{"name":"Early fungi (Ourasphaira-type)","appear_ma":1000,"clade":"fungus","emoji":"🍄","blurb":"Microscopic branching filaments with septa and possible chitin from Arctic Canada represent candidate early fungi, pushing the fungal record deep into the Proterozoic. Such heterotrophs would have decomposed organic matter long before life colonized land.","importance":4,"lat":68,"lng":-132,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":13.25,"plng":160.18},{"name":"Heterotrophic protists","appear_ma":900,"clade":"protist","emoji":"🦷","blurb":"The rise of eukaryotes that eat other cells, evidenced by drill-holes and grazing marks on other microfossils, introduces predation into the microbial world. This ecological innovation would eventually drive the arms races of the Ediacaran and Cambrian.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Cerebrosphaera / large ornamented acritarchs","appear_ma":800,"clade":"protist","emoji":"🌐","blurb":"Large, distinctively wrinkled acritarchs mark a late-Tonian planktic assemblage that later vanishes near the onset of glaciation. Their turnover hints that the placid microbial world was finally beginning to change.","importance":2,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":720,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Testate amoebae (vase-shaped microfossils)","appear_ma":780,"clade":"protist","emoji":"🏺","blurb":"Distinctive flask- and vase-shaped microfossils, famously from the Chuar Group of the Grand Canyon, are the tests of the earliest recognizable amoeboid protists. They provide the first clear evidence of predatory, heterotrophic eukaryotes tunneling into other microfossils.","importance":4,"lat":36.2,"lng":-112.1,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"BoringBillion","plat":-12.46,"plng":96.79},{"name":"Otavia antiqua","common":"Otavia","appear_ma":760,"clade":"animal:sponge","emoji":"🫧","blurb":"Millimetre-sized phosphatic bodies from Namibia have been claimed as the oldest animal fossils, possibly primitive sponges predating the Sturtian freeze, though their biological nature is debated.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":540,"lat":-19,"lng":16,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":-24.9,"plng":49.47},{"name":"Sponges (Porifera)","common":"First sponges","appear_ma":720,"clade":"animal:sponge","emoji":"🧽","blurb":"Molecular clocks and controversial fossils like Otavia from Namibia suggest sponges - the simplest animals, mere colonies of filter-feeding cells - were already present before or during the snowball glaciations.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":-19,"lng":16,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":-27.91,"plng":51.59},{"name":"Acritarchs (large ornamented forms)","common":"Ornamented acritarchs","appear_ma":635,"clade":"protist","emoji":"🔴","blurb":"After the Marinoan meltdown, spiny and ornamented microfossils - probably algal or protist resting cysts - bloomed in the Doushantuo seas, marking a surge in eukaryotic complexity.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":30.8,"lng":111,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":41.03,"plng":44.71},{"name":"Doushantuo embryo-like microfossils","common":"Doushantuo 'embryos'","appear_ma":600,"clade":"protist","emoji":"🥚","blurb":"Exquisitely preserved clusters of dividing cells in Chinese phosphorites were once hailed as the oldest animal embryos, though many are now interpreted as giant sulfur-loving protists.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":551,"lat":30.8,"lng":111,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":26.34,"plng":72.89},{"name":"Multicellular red and green algae","common":"Seaweeds","appear_ma":600,"clade":"plant:algae","emoji":"🌿","blurb":"Macroscopic algae diversified in Ediacaran seas, some like the Chinese Wenghui and Lantian biotas forming leaf- and ribbon-shaped fronds, the first large photosynthetic multicellular life.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":30.8,"lng":111,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":26.34,"plng":72.89},{"name":"Charnia masoni","common":"Charnia","appear_ma":575,"clade":"animal:ediacaran","emoji":"🍃","blurb":"A leaf-shaped rangeomorph anchored to the deep seafloor, Charnia was the first Precambrian complex fossil ever recognised - found by a schoolgirl in England in rocks far too old to hold 'plants'.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":550,"lat":52.7,"lng":-1.3,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":-52.89,"plng":-16.16},{"name":"Rangeomorphs","common":"Rangeomorphs","appear_ma":575,"clade":"animal:ediacaran","emoji":"🌱","blurb":"Fractal, frond-like organisms built from self-similar branching units, rangeomorphs dominated the deep Avalon seas and fed by absorbing nutrients directly from seawater, unlike any animal alive today.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":539,"lat":46.6,"lng":-53.2,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":-57.12,"plng":-11.68},{"name":"Charniodiscus / Arborea","common":"Charniodiscus","appear_ma":575,"clade":"animal:ediacaran","emoji":"🌾","blurb":"A frond on a stalk anchored by a holdfast disc, Charniodiscus swayed in Ediacaran currents like a sea pen, one of the most abundant fossils at Mistaken Point.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":539,"lat":46.6,"lng":-53.2,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":-57.12,"plng":-11.68},{"name":"Dickinsonia","common":"Dickinsonia","appear_ma":567,"clade":"animal:ediacaran","emoji":"🥋","blurb":"A flat, ribbed oval up to a metre long, Dickinsonia is confirmed as an animal by cholesterol biomarkers in its fossils, and left feeding traces as it grazed on microbial mats.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":539,"lat":-30.9,"lng":138.5,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":8.37,"plng":116.04},{"name":"Ediacaran trace makers (first bilaterians)","common":"First burrowers","appear_ma":560,"clade":"animal:ediacaran","emoji":"〰️","blurb":"Simple horizontal trails and shallow burrows appear in latest Ediacaran rocks, the tracks of the earliest muscular, bilaterally symmetric animals moving under their own power.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":66,"lng":40,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":-23.75,"plng":-100.54},{"name":"Kimberella","common":"Kimberella","appear_ma":558,"clade":"animal:mollusc","emoji":"🐌","blurb":"A slug-like creature that scraped microbial mats with a rasping organ, Kimberella is the best candidate for an early bilaterian - possibly a stem-mollusc - showing that muscular, mobile animals existed.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":539,"lat":66,"lng":40,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":-23.75,"plng":-100.54},{"name":"Spriggina","common":"Spriggina","appear_ma":558,"clade":"animal:ediacaran","emoji":"🪶","blurb":"A segmented, bilaterally symmetric organism resembling a feather or a primitive arthropod, Spriggina hints at the body plans that would soon flourish - though its exact affinities remain contested.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":539,"lat":-30.9,"lng":138.5,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":4.37,"plng":111.54},{"name":"Tribrachidium","common":"Tribrachidium","appear_ma":555,"clade":"animal:ediacaran","emoji":"🔱","blurb":"A disc with three spiralling arms, Tribrachidium had a bizarre three-fold symmetry found in no living animal, and fluid-flow models show it was a stationary suspension feeder.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":539,"lat":-30.9,"lng":138.5,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":4.37,"plng":111.54},{"name":"Palaeophragmodictya","common":"Early demosponge","appear_ma":555,"clade":"animal:sponge","emoji":"🧼","blurb":"Disc-shaped impressions from the Ediacara Hills preserve a possible sponge with a spicule skeleton, evidence that recognisable sponges lived among the strange Ediacaran fauna.","importance":2,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":-30.9,"lng":138.5,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":4.37,"plng":111.54},{"name":"Cloudina","common":"Cloudina","appear_ma":550,"clade":"animal:cnidarian","emoji":"🐚","blurb":"Stacked cone-in-cone carbonate tubes, Cloudina built the first animal skeletons and bear the earliest evidence of predation - tiny boreholes drilled by an unknown attacker.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":538,"lat":-25,"lng":16,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":-49.88,"plng":151.81},{"name":"Namacalathus","common":"Namacalathus","appear_ma":548,"clade":"animal:cnidarian","emoji":"🏺","blurb":"A goblet-shaped, mineralized organism on a stalk, Namacalathus built reefs alongside Cloudina in the terminal Ediacaran and may be an early relative of corals or lophophorates.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":538,"lat":-25,"lng":16,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran","plat":-49.88,"plng":151.81},{"name":"Brachiopoda","common":"Brachiopods (lamp shells)","appear_ma":530,"clade":"animal:mollusc","emoji":"🦪","blurb":"Two-shelled filter-feeders that anchored to the seafloor and dominated Paleozoic benthic communities, still surviving today in deep and cold waters.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Echinodermata (early)","common":"Early echinoderms","appear_ma":525,"clade":"animal:echinoderm","emoji":"⭐","blurb":"Ancestors of sea stars and urchins first appeared as bizarre asymmetric and stalked forms before settling on the fivefold symmetry that defines the group today.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Cambrian sponges (Archaeocyatha)","common":"Archaeocyathids","appear_ma":525,"clade":"animal:sponge","emoji":"🧽","blurb":"Cup-shaped sponge relatives that built the first animal reefs in early Cambrian seas before vanishing, a brief but pioneering reef-building experiment.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":510,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Trilobita","common":"Trilobites","appear_ma":521,"clade":"animal:arthropod","emoji":"🦂","blurb":"Armored, three-lobed arthropods that scuttled across Paleozoic seafloors for 270 million years, evolving some of the first sophisticated compound eyes made of calcite lenses.","importance":5,"lat":24.7,"lng":102.9,"extinct_ma":251.9,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":-20.86,"plng":97.69},{"name":"Conodonta","common":"Conodonts","appear_ma":520,"clade":"vertebrate:fish","emoji":"🦷","blurb":"Eel-like jawless vertebrates known mainly from their tooth-like phosphate elements, the sharpest microscopic teeth ever measured and a geologist's favorite dating tool.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":201.4,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Anomalocaris","common":"Anomalocaris","appear_ma":518,"clade":"animal:arthropod","emoji":"🦑","blurb":"A meter-long apex predator that swam by rippling side flaps and seized prey with a pair of spiny grasping appendages beneath a disc-shaped, tooth-ringed mouth.","importance":5,"lat":51.4,"lng":-116.5,"extinct_ma":500,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":51.4,"plng":-116.5},{"name":"Haikouichthys","common":"Haikouichthys","appear_ma":518,"clade":"vertebrate:fish","emoji":"🐟","blurb":"A finger-length, jawless swimmer from Chengjiang with a notochord, gills, and rudimentary vertebrae, ranking among the earliest known vertebrates.","importance":5,"lat":24.7,"lng":102.9,"extinct_ma":510,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":-20.86,"plng":97.69},{"name":"Myllokunmingia","common":"Myllokunmingia","appear_ma":518,"clade":"vertebrate:fish","emoji":"🐠","blurb":"One of the oldest known fish, this tiny Cambrian craniate already had a head with paired eyes, gill pouches, and a fin fold running along its back.","importance":5,"lat":24.7,"lng":102.9,"extinct_ma":510,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":-20.86,"plng":97.69},{"name":"Wiwaxia","common":"Wiwaxia","appear_ma":515,"clade":"animal:mollusc","emoji":"🛡️","blurb":"A slug-like seafloor grazer armored in overlapping scales and upright spines, offering clues to the shared ancestry of molluscs and annelid worms.","importance":3,"lat":51.4,"lng":-116.5,"extinct_ma":500,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":51.4,"plng":-116.5},{"name":"Graptolithina","common":"Graptolites","appear_ma":515,"clade":"animal:cnidarian","emoji":"✒️","blurb":"Colonial plankton whose fossilized saw-blade skeletons look like pencil marks on rock and are so precise in time that geologists use them to date Paleozoic strata.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":320,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Opabinia","common":"Opabinia","appear_ma":508,"clade":"animal:arthropod","emoji":"🐛","blurb":"A bizarre Burgess Shale animal with five stalked eyes and a single flexible, clawed proboscis it used like a hose to grab food and pass it to its mouth.","importance":4,"lat":51.4,"lng":-116.5,"extinct_ma":500,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":51.4,"plng":-116.5},{"name":"Hallucigenia","common":"Hallucigenia","appear_ma":508,"clade":"animal:arthropod","emoji":"🐍","blurb":"A worm-like lobopodian so strange that early reconstructions placed it upside-down and backwards; it walked on soft legs beneath two rows of defensive spines.","importance":4,"lat":51.4,"lng":-116.5,"extinct_ma":500,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":51.4,"plng":-116.5},{"name":"Pikaia","common":"Pikaia","appear_ma":508,"clade":"vertebrate:fish","emoji":"🎗️","blurb":"A ribbon-like Burgess Shale chordate that swam by flexing a stiffening notochord, long celebrated as a relative of our own earliest ancestors.","importance":3,"lat":51.4,"lng":-116.5,"extinct_ma":500,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":51.4,"plng":-116.5},{"name":"Marrella","common":"Marrella (lace crab)","appear_ma":508,"clade":"animal:arthropod","emoji":"🦋","blurb":"The most abundant fossil in the Burgess Shale, a delicate, lacy arthropod with two pairs of swept-back head spines that shimmered iridescently in life.","importance":2,"lat":51.4,"lng":-116.5,"extinct_ma":500,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":51.4,"plng":-116.5},{"name":"Cephalopoda (orthocones)","common":"Straight-shelled cephalopods","appear_ma":490,"clade":"animal:mollusc","emoji":"🐚","blurb":"Squid relatives housed in long, straight, chambered cones that jetted through Ordovician seas as the era's largest and fiercest active predators.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Crinoidea","common":"Crinoids (sea lilies)","appear_ma":480,"clade":"animal:echinoderm","emoji":"🌸","blurb":"Stalked, flower-like echinoderms that radiated during the Ordovician to carpet the seafloor as filter-feeding meadows, with relatives still alive today.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Early land fungi","common":"First terrestrial fungi","appear_ma":470,"clade":"fungus","emoji":"🍄","blurb":"Fungi likely partnered with the earliest land plants, helping them draw nutrients from bare rock and easing the pioneering colonization of the continents.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Cryptospore-producing land plants","common":"First land plants","appear_ma":470,"clade":"plant:land","emoji":"🌿","blurb":"Low, moss- and liverwort-grade plants without roots or vascular tissue that hugged wet ground, releasing the oldest known land-plant spores.","importance":5,"lat":-22.5,"lng":-65,"extinct_ma":null,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":-29.42,"plng":-164.53},{"name":"Astraspis / Arandaspis","common":"Early armored jawless fish","appear_ma":470,"clade":"vertebrate:fish","emoji":"🐡","blurb":"Among the first vertebrates with bony armor, these jawless fish encased their heads in mineralized plates, marking an early experiment in the origin of bone.","importance":3,"lat":-31,"lng":-66,"extinct_ma":443,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician","plat":-23.0,"plng":-170.83},{"name":"Rugose corals","appear_ma":460,"extinct_ma":251.9,"clade":"animal:cnidarian","common":"Horn coral","emoji":"🪸","blurb":"Solitary horn-shaped and colonial corals that built Paleozoic reefs alongside tabulate corals; both entire coral orders were extinguished at the end of the Permian and modern corals arose from different ancestors.","importance":2,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Tabulate & rugose corals / stromatoporoids","common":"Devonian reef-builders","appear_ma":440,"extinct_ma":359,"clade":"animal:cnidarian","emoji":"🪸","blurb":"Colonial tabulate and horn-shaped rugose corals, together with sponge-like stromatoporoids, built the vast reef systems of the Devonian, the largest the world had yet seen. The Late Devonian extinctions shattered these reefs almost completely.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Cooksonia","common":"Cooksonia","appear_ma":430,"extinct_ma":393,"clade":"plant:land","emoji":"🌿","blurb":"A tiny leafless, rootless plant, just a few centimetres tall, whose forked green stems ended in trumpet-shaped spore capsules. It is one of the earliest vascular plants and an icon of the greening of the land.","importance":5,"lat":52,"lng":-2.6,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-18.78,"plng":-71.76},{"name":"Prototaxites","common":"Giant fungus","appear_ma":420,"extinct_ma":370,"clade":"fungus","emoji":"🍄","blurb":"Towering columns up to 8 metres tall, Prototaxites were the largest organisms on land for millions of years and are now thought to be gigantic fungi. They loomed over a landscape whose plants barely reached the knee.","importance":4,"lat":45.5,"lng":-64.3,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-30.22,"plng":-76.11},{"name":"Trigonotarbid arachnids","common":"Early arachnids","appear_ma":414,"extinct_ma":290,"clade":"animal:arthropod","emoji":"🕷️","blurb":"Spider-like predators with segmented, armored bodies, trigonotarbids hunted through the earliest land ecosystems though they lacked silk glands. They are among the first arachnids to live entirely on land.","importance":3,"lat":57.4,"lng":-2.6,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-20.28,"plng":-40.95},{"name":"Pneumodesmus / early millipedes","common":"First air-breathing land animal","appear_ma":414,"extinct_ma":null,"clade":"animal:arthropod","emoji":"🐛","blurb":"Pneumodesmus, a Silurian millipede from Scotland, preserves spiracles, the breathing holes of an air-breathing animal, making it a contender for the oldest known creature to breathe on land. Myriapods were pioneers of the terrestrial soil fauna.","importance":4,"lat":56.6,"lng":-2.5,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-20.85,"plng":-40.35},{"name":"Rhynia","common":"Rhynia","appear_ma":410,"extinct_ma":400,"clade":"plant:land","emoji":"🌱","blurb":"Known in cellular detail from the Rhynie chert, Rhynie was a simple leafless plant of naked green stems with underground rhizomes and spore capsules. It reveals the basic body plan of early vascular land plants.","importance":4,"lat":57.4,"lng":-2.6,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-20.28,"plng":-40.95},{"name":"Coelacanth","common":"Coelacanth","appear_ma":410,"extinct_ma":null,"clade":"vertebrate:fish","emoji":"🎣","blurb":"A lobe-finned fish whose fleshy, limb-like fins hint at the ancestry of land vertebrates; declared long extinct, a living coelacanth stunned scientists when caught in 1938. It remains a celebrated living fossil.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Lungfish (Dipnoi)","common":"Lungfish","appear_ma":410,"extinct_ma":null,"clade":"vertebrate:fish","emoji":"🫁","blurb":"Devonian lungfish could gulp air with primitive lungs and, like their living descendants, survive stagnant water. They are among the closest fish relatives of the tetrapods that walked ashore.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Rhyniognatha / early insects","common":"First insects","appear_ma":410,"extinct_ma":null,"clade":"animal:arthropod","emoji":"🐜","blurb":"Preserved fragments from the Rhynie chert include what may be the oldest known insect, complete with insect-style jaws. Insects would go on to become the most diverse animal lineage on Earth.","importance":4,"lat":57.4,"lng":-2.6,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-20.28,"plng":-40.95},{"name":"Ammonoids","common":"Ammonoids","appear_ma":409,"extinct_ma":66,"clade":"animal:mollusc","emoji":"🐚","blurb":"Coiled, chambered cephalopods that jetted through Devonian seas, ammonoids evolved so rapidly that geologists use them to date rocks to within a few hundred thousand years. Their dynasty ran until the dinosaur-killing impact.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Goniatite ammonoids","appear_ma":390,"extinct_ma":251.9,"clade":"animal:mollusc","common":"Goniatite","emoji":"🐚","blurb":"Coiled, shelled cephalopods with simple zig-zag suture lines that were fast-evolving and abundant, making them prized index fossils; nearly all ammonoid lineages were annihilated in the end-Permian crisis.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Archaeopteris","common":"First true tree","appear_ma":385,"extinct_ma":358,"clade":"plant:land","emoji":"🌲","blurb":"A tall progymnosperm with real wood, fern-like fronds and a deep root system, Archaeopteris built Earth's first widespread forests. Its roots and leaf-fall drew down CO2 and helped cool the planet.","importance":5,"lat":42.6,"lng":-74.1,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-31.35,"plng":-58.38},{"name":"Eusthenopteron","common":"Eusthenopteron","appear_ma":385,"extinct_ma":374,"clade":"vertebrate:fish","emoji":"🐠","blurb":"A predatory lobe-finned fish with limb-like fin bones arranged like a proto-arm, Eusthenopteron sits just below tetrapods on the family tree. It shows the skeletal blueprint of a limb already present in water.","importance":4,"lat":48.6,"lng":-64.3,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-28.25,"plng":-48.52},{"name":"Dunkleosteus","common":"Dunkleosteus","appear_ma":382,"extinct_ma":358,"clade":"vertebrate:fish","emoji":"🦈","blurb":"A monstrous armored placoderm up to 6 metres long, Dunkleosteus had no teeth but self-sharpening bony blades and one of the most powerful bites ever measured in a fish. It was the apex predator of the Late Devonian seas.","importance":5,"lat":41.5,"lng":-81.7,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-29.35,"plng":-64.62},{"name":"Cladoselache","common":"Early shark","appear_ma":380,"extinct_ma":360,"clade":"vertebrate:fish","emoji":"🐟","blurb":"A streamlined, fast-swimming early shark about 2 metres long with keeled tail fins, preserved so well in Ohio shale that its muscles and last meals survive. It shows sharks were already sleek predators in the Devonian.","importance":4,"lat":41.5,"lng":-81.7,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-29.35,"plng":-64.62},{"name":"Tiktaalik","common":"Tiktaalik","appear_ma":375,"extinct_ma":374,"clade":"vertebrate:fish","emoji":"🐊","blurb":"A flat-headed fishapod with a mobile neck, lungs and wrist-jointed fins, Tiktaalik could prop itself up in shallow water like a push-up. It is the celebrated intermediate between fish and four-legged animals.","importance":5,"lat":76,"lng":-88,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-0.28,"plng":-46.14},{"name":"Acanthostega","common":"Acanthostega","appear_ma":365,"extinct_ma":360,"clade":"vertebrate:tetrapod","emoji":"🦎","blurb":"One of the earliest four-limbed vertebrates, Acanthostega had eight fingers on each hand yet still breathed through internal gills, so its limbs likely evolved for moving in water before walking. It reveals that legs came before land.","importance":5,"lat":72,"lng":-24,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-9.15,"plng":-22.94},{"name":"Ichthyostega","common":"Ichthyostega","appear_ma":365,"extinct_ma":360,"clade":"vertebrate:tetrapod","emoji":"🐾","blurb":"A robust early tetrapod from Greenland with a stout ribcage and paddle-tailed hindlimbs, Ichthyostega could probably haul itself over land like a mudskipper or seal. It is a key figure in the first steps onto shore.","importance":5,"lat":72,"lng":-24,"era":"Silurian-Devonian","plat":-9.15,"plng":-22.94},{"name":"Ferns and tree ferns","common":"Ferns (Mesozoic ground cover)","appear_ma":360,"extinct_ma":null,"clade":"plant:land","emoji":"🌿","blurb":"Ferns carpeted the Triassic and Jurassic undergrowth and, as tree ferns, formed part of the canopy. Fast-spreading 'fern spikes' also marked the disturbed ground in the wake of the mass extinctions.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Lepidodendron","appear_ma":359,"extinct_ma":298,"clade":"plant:land","common":"Scale tree","emoji":"🌴","blurb":"A towering lycopsid up to 40 m tall whose trunk was armored in diamond-shaped leaf scars, it grew fast in the coal swamps, reproduced with spores, then died after shedding its crown, its remains forming much of the world's coal.","importance":5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Sigillaria","appear_ma":359,"extinct_ma":305,"clade":"plant:land","common":"Seal tree","emoji":"🌵","blurb":"A giant lycopsid relative of Lepidodendron with a stouter, often unbranched trunk marked by vertical rows of leaf scars, a signature tree of the Carboniferous coal forests.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Calamites","appear_ma":359,"extinct_ma":251,"clade":"plant:land","common":"Giant horsetail","emoji":"🎋","blurb":"A tree-sized horsetail up to 20 m tall with jointed, hollow, bamboo-like stems that grew along swamp margins, its living relatives are the small horsetails (Equisetum) still found by streams today.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Medullosa (seed ferns)","appear_ma":359,"extinct_ma":251,"clade":"plant:land","common":"Seed fern","emoji":"🌿","blurb":"Fern-like plants that reproduced with true seeds rather than spores, bridging ferns and later seed plants and producing large fronds that carpeted the Carboniferous forest floor.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Arthropleura","appear_ma":345,"extinct_ma":290,"clade":"animal:arthropod","common":"Giant millipede","emoji":"🐛","blurb":"The largest land invertebrate ever, a millipede relative up to 2.5 m long that trundled through the coal forests eating plant litter, its meter-wide trackways preserved in Carboniferous sandstones.","importance":5,"lat":55,"lng":-3.5,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":-3.47,"plng":0.26},{"name":"Fusulinids","appear_ma":340,"extinct_ma":251.9,"clade":"protist","common":"Fusulinid foram","emoji":"🌾","blurb":"Rice-grain-shaped single-celled foraminifera that built intricate coiled shells and swarmed the warm Permian seafloor in such numbers they form whole limestones; they were wiped out entirely at the Great Dying.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Pulmonoscorpius","appear_ma":336,"extinct_ma":330,"clade":"animal:arthropod","common":"Giant scorpion","emoji":"🦂","blurb":"A land scorpion up to 70 cm long from the early Carboniferous of Scotland, a giant predator taking advantage of high oxygen and abundant arthropod prey.","importance":2,"lat":56,"lng":-3.7,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":-2.59,"plng":-0.23},{"name":"Hylonomus","appear_ma":318,"extinct_ma":312,"clade":"vertebrate:reptile","common":"Earliest reptile","emoji":"🦎","blurb":"A slender, 20 cm lizard-like animal found fossilized inside hollow Lepidodendron stumps at Joggins, Nova Scotia, it is among the oldest known true reptiles and a founding amniote.","importance":5,"lat":45.7,"lng":-64.4,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":-5.79,"plng":-3.68},{"name":"Meganeura","appear_ma":317,"extinct_ma":299,"clade":"animal:arthropod","common":"Giant dragonfly","emoji":"🦟","blurb":"A griffinfly with a wingspan up to 70 cm, the largest flying insect ever, its enormous size made possible by the oxygen-rich Carboniferous air; it hunted other insects over the coal swamps.","importance":5,"lat":46.1,"lng":2.4,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":-3.52,"plng":21.14},{"name":"Walchia (early conifer)","appear_ma":310,"extinct_ma":251,"clade":"plant:land","common":"Voltzialean conifer","emoji":"🌲","blurb":"One of the first true conifers, a drought-tolerant evergreen with small scale-like needles that thrived in the drier uplands and heralded the seed-plant world of the Permian and beyond.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Archaeothyris","appear_ma":306,"extinct_ma":306,"clade":"vertebrate:synapsid","common":"Early synapsid","emoji":"🦎","blurb":"One of the oldest known synapsids, also from Nova Scotia, its single skull opening behind the eye marks the very base of the lineage that would lead through pelycosaurs and therapsids to mammals.","importance":4,"lat":45.7,"lng":-64.4,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":-2.01,"plng":-2.87},{"name":"Edaphosaurus","appear_ma":300,"extinct_ma":280,"clade":"vertebrate:synapsid","common":"Sailed herbivore","emoji":"🦕","blurb":"A plant-eating pelycosaur that, like Dimetrodon, bore a tall back sail but studded with cross-bars; it was one of the first large land herbivores adapted to chewing bulky vegetation.","importance":3,"lat":33.6,"lng":-98.7,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":-1.27,"plng":-30.97},{"name":"Conifers","common":"Conifers (Mesozoic forests)","appear_ma":300,"extinct_ma":null,"clade":"plant:land","emoji":"🌲","blurb":"Cone-bearing trees such as araucarias and early relatives of redwoods and pines formed the towering forests that fed the giant sauropods. They were the dominant large plants of the Jurassic landscape.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Glossopteris","appear_ma":299,"extinct_ma":251,"clade":"plant:land","common":"Glossopteris seed fern","emoji":"🍃","blurb":"A tongue-leaved seed fern that dominated cool, wet Gondwana across South America, Africa, India, Australia and Antarctica; its identical fossils on now-separated continents were early proof of continental drift.","importance":4,"lat":-77,"lng":160,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":-76.77,"plng":158.7},{"name":"Dimetrodon","appear_ma":295,"extinct_ma":272,"clade":"vertebrate:synapsid","common":"Sail-backed pelycosaur","emoji":"🦖","blurb":"An early Permian apex predator up to 4.5 m long famous for the tall skin sail on its back, it was a synapsid far closer to mammals than to dinosaurs, which had not yet appeared.","importance":5,"lat":33.6,"lng":-98.7,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":-1.27,"plng":-30.97},{"name":"Eryops","appear_ma":295,"extinct_ma":270,"clade":"vertebrate:amphibian","common":"Giant amphibian","emoji":"🐊","blurb":"A robust, 2 m crocodile-like temnospondyl amphibian that lurked in Permian swamps and rivers, one of the largest land animals of its time before amniotes took over.","importance":3,"lat":33.6,"lng":-98.7,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":-1.27,"plng":-30.97},{"name":"Mesosaurus","appear_ma":290,"extinct_ma":270,"clade":"vertebrate:reptile","common":"Freshwater reptile","emoji":"🦎","blurb":"A small, long-snouted aquatic reptile whose identical fossils turn up in Permian rocks of both Brazil and southern Africa, another line of evidence that these lands were once joined in Pangaea.","importance":3,"lat":-27,"lng":-52,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":-46.63,"plng":-32.18},{"name":"Cycads","common":"Cycads (Mesozoic palm-like plants)","appear_ma":265,"extinct_ma":null,"clade":"plant:land","emoji":"🌴","blurb":"Stout, palm-like seed plants with crowns of tough fronds, cycads were so abundant in the Jurassic that the period is nicknamed the 'Age of Cycads.' A few relict species still survive in the tropics today.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Gorgonops","appear_ma":260,"extinct_ma":251.9,"clade":"vertebrate:synapsid","common":"Gorgonopsian","emoji":"🐅","blurb":"A saber-toothed therapsid the size of a bear that was the top predator of the late Permian Karoo, its more upright limbs and specialized teeth showing the synapsid march toward mammals.","importance":4,"lat":-32,"lng":24,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":-58.1,"plng":-27.54},{"name":"Dicynodon","appear_ma":259,"extinct_ma":251.9,"clade":"vertebrate:synapsid","common":"Tusked dicynodont","emoji":"🦛","blurb":"A stocky, beaked, tusk-bearing plant-eating therapsid that was one of the most abundant land vertebrates of the late Permian before the Great Dying erased most dicynodonts.","importance":3,"lat":-32,"lng":24,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":-58.1,"plng":-27.54},{"name":"Lystrosaurus","appear_ma":255,"extinct_ma":250,"clade":"vertebrate:synapsid","common":"Disaster survivor","emoji":"🐗","blurb":"A pig-sized, barrel-bodied dicynodont with two tusks and a horny beak that famously survived the end-Permian catastrophe and then swarmed the empty early Triassic world, at times making up most of all land vertebrate fossils.","importance":4,"lat":-32,"lng":24,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian","plat":-58.1,"plng":-27.54},{"name":"Ichthyosaurs","common":"Ichthyosaurs (fish-lizards)","appear_ma":249,"extinct_ma":90,"clade":"vertebrate:reptile","emoji":"🐬","blurb":"These dolphin-shaped marine reptiles ranged from Triassic giants over 15 metres long to sleek, large-eyed Jurassic hunters, and gave birth to live young at sea. Superbly streamlined, they were the ocean's premier predators for tens of millions of years.","importance":5,"lat":50.7,"lng":-2.9,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":18.94,"plng":21.64},{"name":"Cynognathus","common":"Cynognathus (dog-jaw cynodont)","appear_ma":247,"extinct_ma":237,"clade":"vertebrate:synapsid","emoji":"🐕","blurb":"A wolf-sized cynodont with powerful, mammal-like differentiated teeth, Cynognathus was an apex predator of the Early-Middle Triassic. As an advanced synapsid it sits close to the ancestry of true mammals.","importance":3,"lat":-32,"lng":24,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":-57.64,"plng":-20.97},{"name":"Ammonites","common":"Ammonites (coiled cephalopods)","appear_ma":240,"extinct_ma":66,"clade":"animal:mollusc","emoji":"🐚","blurb":"Fast-evolving coiled cephalopods, ammonites diversified explosively after the end-Triassic crisis and ruled Jurassic seas as swimming predators. Their rapidly changing shell forms make them prized index fossils for dating rocks.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Belemnites","common":"Belemnites (bullet squid)","appear_ma":240,"extinct_ma":66,"clade":"animal:mollusc","emoji":"🦑","blurb":"Squid-like cephalopods with an internal bullet-shaped skeleton, belemnites swarmed the Jurassic seas in vast numbers. Their hard 'guards' are among the most common Mesozoic marine fossils.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Scleractinian (stony) corals","common":"Modern stony corals","appear_ma":240,"extinct_ma":null,"clade":"animal:cnidarian","emoji":"🪸","blurb":"The modern lineage of reef-building stony corals arose in the Middle Triassic after older Paleozoic corals died out. By the Jurassic they were constructing extensive reefs in the warm shelf seas of the Tethys.","importance":3,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Eoraptor lunensis","common":"Eoraptor (one of the first dinosaurs)","appear_ma":231,"extinct_ma":228,"clade":"vertebrate:dinosaur","emoji":"🦖","blurb":"A metre-long, lightly built biped from Argentina's Ischigualasto Formation, Eoraptor is among the oldest known dinosaurs. Its mix of meat- and plant-suited teeth hints at the omnivorous roots of the dinosaur radiation.","importance":5,"lat":-30.16,"lng":-67.99,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":-42.98,"plng":-30.96},{"name":"Herrerasaurus ischigualastensis","common":"Herrerasaurus (early predatory dinosaur)","appear_ma":231,"extinct_ma":228,"clade":"vertebrate:dinosaur","emoji":"🦖","blurb":"A three-to-six-metre carnivore from Late Triassic Argentina, Herrerasaurus was already a capable hunter with a hinged lower jaw and grasping hands. It shows that predatory dinosaurs were formidable from very near their origin.","importance":5,"lat":-30.16,"lng":-67.99,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":-42.98,"plng":-30.96},{"name":"Eudimorphodon","common":"Eudimorphodon (early pterosaur)","appear_ma":228,"extinct_ma":201,"clade":"vertebrate:reptile","emoji":"🪽","blurb":"One of the earliest well-known pterosaurs, this Late Triassic flier from Italy had a metre-wide wingspan and a mouthful of over 100 specialised teeth for catching fish. Its long tail ended in a small vane for steering.","importance":4,"lat":45.7,"lng":9.7,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":16.06,"plng":26.6},{"name":"Plateosaurus","common":"Plateosaurus (early giant plant-eater)","appear_ma":214,"extinct_ma":204,"clade":"vertebrate:dinosaur","emoji":"🦕","blurb":"One of the first truly large dinosaurs at up to 8–10 metres, this Late Triassic European sauropodomorph browsed on high vegetation, sometimes rearing on its hind legs. Its herds are known from bone-rich European quarries.","importance":4,"lat":49.8,"lng":9.9,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":36.19,"plng":21.63},{"name":"Coelophysis","common":"Coelophysis (agile Triassic predator)","appear_ma":214,"extinct_ma":201,"clade":"vertebrate:dinosaur","emoji":"🦖","blurb":"A slender, fast, hollow-boned theropod known from hundreds of individuals at Ghost Ranch, New Mexico, Coelophysis hunted small prey in packs across the Late Triassic. It is one of the best-understood early dinosaurs.","importance":4,"lat":36.3,"lng":-106.3,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":8.54,"plng":-36.21},{"name":"Proganochelys","common":"Proganochelys (early turtle)","appear_ma":210,"extinct_ma":201,"clade":"vertebrate:reptile","emoji":"🐢","blurb":"One of the oldest true turtles, this Late Triassic German reptile already had a fully formed bony shell, though it could not yet pull its head inside. Spikes on its neck and tail provided extra armour.","importance":3,"lat":51.8,"lng":10.6,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":37.91,"plng":20.23},{"name":"Morganucodon","common":"Morganucodon (one of the first mammals)","appear_ma":205,"extinct_ma":190,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🐭","blurb":"A shrew-sized, insect-eating mammaliaform from the Late Triassic and Early Jurassic, Morganucodon had the new dentary-squamosal jaw joint that defines mammals. Nocturnal and likely furry, it typifies the small, hidden lives of the earliest mammals.","importance":5,"lat":51.5,"lng":-3,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":34.16,"plng":14.28},{"name":"Plesiosaurs","common":"Plesiosaurs (long-necked sea reptiles)","appear_ma":203,"extinct_ma":66,"clade":"vertebrate:reptile","emoji":"🦕","blurb":"Marine reptiles that 'flew' through the water on four wing-like flippers, plesiosaurs included absurdly long-necked forms and short-necked, big-headed pliosaur predators. They cruised the Jurassic seas from the earliest Jurassic onward.","importance":4,"lat":50.7,"lng":-2.9,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":33.57,"plng":14.93},{"name":"Ginkgo","common":"Ginkgo (maidenhair tree)","appear_ma":200,"extinct_ma":null,"clade":"plant:land","emoji":"🍂","blurb":"With distinctive fan-shaped leaves, ginkgos flourished across the Mesozoic and remain today as a single 'living fossil' species, Ginkgo biloba. A Jurassic Ginkgo leaf is nearly indistinguishable from one you can pick up in a modern city park.","importance":4,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Allosaurus","common":"Allosaurus (top Jurassic predator)","appear_ma":155,"extinct_ma":145,"clade":"vertebrate:dinosaur","emoji":"🦖","blurb":"The dominant large carnivore of the Late Jurassic Morrison Formation, this 8–9 metre theropod had bladed, serrated teeth and a wide-gaping jaw for slashing at giant prey. It is one of the most abundant and best-known predatory dinosaurs.","importance":5,"lat":40.2,"lng":-108,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":30.48,"plng":-36.84},{"name":"Stegosaurus","common":"Stegosaurus (plated dinosaur)","appear_ma":155,"extinct_ma":145,"clade":"vertebrate:dinosaur","emoji":"🦖","blurb":"Instantly recognisable for the double row of tall bony plates along its back and a spiked 'thagomizer' tail, Stegosaurus was a low-browsing Late Jurassic herbivore. Its plates likely served in display and heat regulation more than defence.","importance":5,"lat":40.2,"lng":-108,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":30.48,"plng":-36.84},{"name":"Brachiosaurus","common":"Brachiosaurus (giraffe-necked giant)","appear_ma":154,"extinct_ma":150,"clade":"vertebrate:dinosaur","emoji":"🦕","blurb":"A colossal Late Jurassic sauropod with forelegs longer than its hindlegs, Brachiosaurus held its head high to browse treetops like a giraffe. It weighed on the order of 30–50 tonnes and roamed the Morrison floodplains of North America.","importance":5,"lat":40.2,"lng":-108,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":37.48,"plng":-35.49},{"name":"Diplodocus","common":"Diplodocus (whip-tailed giant)","appear_ma":154,"extinct_ma":150,"clade":"vertebrate:dinosaur","emoji":"🦕","blurb":"One of the longest dinosaurs at up to 26–33 metres, Diplodocus balanced an immensely long neck with a whip-like tail. Its peg-like teeth stripped soft plants from low, sweeping vegetation across Late Jurassic North America.","importance":5,"lat":40.2,"lng":-108,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":37.48,"plng":-35.49},{"name":"Archaeopteryx","common":"Archaeopteryx (first bird)","appear_ma":150,"extinct_ma":148,"clade":"vertebrate:bird","emoji":"🪶","blurb":"A crow-sized feathered creature from the Solnhofen limestone, Archaeopteryx blended bird-like wings and flight feathers with reptilian teeth, clawed fingers, and a long bony tail. It is the iconic transitional fossil linking dinosaurs to birds.","importance":5,"lat":48.9,"lng":11.2,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":31.09,"plng":37.9},{"name":"Compsognathus","common":"Compsognathus (chicken-sized theropod)","appear_ma":150,"extinct_ma":148,"clade":"vertebrate:dinosaur","emoji":"🦎","blurb":"One of the smallest known dinosaurs, this turkey-sized Late Jurassic predator from Germany and France darted after lizards on two long legs. It underscores how close small theropods were to the first birds.","importance":3,"lat":48.9,"lng":11.2,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic","plat":31.09,"plng":37.9},{"name":"Ammonites (final radiation)","common":"Ammonites","appear_ma":145,"clade":"animal:mollusc","emoji":"🐚","blurb":"Coil-shelled cephalopods filled Cretaceous seas in dazzling variety, from tightly wound spirals to bizarre uncoiled forms, before vanishing entirely at the K-Pg boundary.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":66,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Enantiornithine birds","common":"Opposite birds","appear_ma":130,"clade":"vertebrate:bird","emoji":"🐦","blurb":"The dominant birds of the Cretaceous, these toothed 'opposite birds' diversified alongside dinosaurs but died out at the K-Pg boundary, leaving no descendants.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":66,"lat":41.6,"lng":121,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":50.79,"plng":141.49},{"name":"Archaefructus","common":"Archaefructus","appear_ma":125,"clade":"plant:flower","emoji":"🌿","blurb":"An aquatic herb from China's Yixian Formation, this is among the earliest well-preserved flowering plants — bearing seeds and stamens but no showy petals.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":41.6,"lng":121,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":48.55,"plng":141.8},{"name":"Eomaia scansoria","common":"Eomaia","appear_ma":125,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🐁","blurb":"A shrew-sized climber from China, Eomaia is one of the earliest known placental-line mammals, hinting that our own reproductive strategy is over 100 million years old.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":41.6,"lng":121,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":48.55,"plng":141.8},{"name":"Early marsupials (metatherians)","common":"Early marsupials","appear_ma":110,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🐨","blurb":"Small marsupial-line mammals like Sinodelphys and Didelphodon scurried through the Cretaceous underbrush, giving rise to the pouched mammals of today's Americas and Australia.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Magnolia (early magnoliids)","common":"Magnolia and first true flowers","appear_ma":100,"clade":"plant:flower","emoji":"🌷","blurb":"Big, simple, beetle-pollinated blossoms like early magnolias represent some of the first recognizably 'flowery' flowers, an ancient lineage whose descendants still bloom today.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Apidae (early bees)","common":"First bees","appear_ma":100,"clade":"animal:arthropod","emoji":"🐝","blurb":"Bees evolve from wasp ancestors as flowers proliferate, striking up the pollination partnership that would reshape terrestrial ecosystems; the oldest known bee is preserved in amber.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Mosasaurs","common":"Mosasaurs","appear_ma":100,"clade":"vertebrate:reptile","emoji":"🐍","blurb":"Enormous marine lizards related to snakes and monitors, mosasaurs like Mosasaurus and Tylosaurus ruled the Cretaceous seas as top predators after the ichthyosaurs faded.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":66,"lat":50.8,"lng":5.7,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":42.85,"plng":21.76},{"name":"Titanosaurs","common":"Titanosaurs","appear_ma":100,"clade":"vertebrate:dinosaur","emoji":"🦕","blurb":"The last and largest sauropods, titanosaurs like Argentinosaurus reached 30+ meters and roamed Gondwana, laying clutches of eggs in vast nesting grounds.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":66,"lat":-38,"lng":-68,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":-39.25,"plng":-34.17},{"name":"Spinosaurus aegyptiacus","common":"Spinosaurus","appear_ma":99,"clade":"vertebrate:dinosaur","emoji":"🐊","blurb":"A sail-backed, crocodile-snouted giant from North Africa that hunted fish in river systems, likely the largest predatory dinosaur and possibly semi-aquatic.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":94,"lat":30,"lng":-5,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":21.37,"plng":5.93},{"name":"Hadrosaurs (duck-billed dinosaurs)","common":"Duck-billed dinosaurs","appear_ma":90,"clade":"vertebrate:dinosaur","emoji":"🦆","blurb":"Herds of duck-billed herbivores like Edmontosaurus and crested Parasaurolophus dominated late-Cretaceous floodplains, chewing tough plants with hundreds of grinding teeth.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":66,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Ichthyornis and Hesperornis","common":"Toothed seabirds","appear_ma":90,"clade":"vertebrate:bird","emoji":"🐧","blurb":"Ichthyornis was a tern-like flyer and Hesperornis a flightless diving bird — both toothed seabirds close to the ancestry of modern birds that patrolled Cretaceous coasts.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":66,"lat":39,"lng":-99,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":42.29,"plng":-56.27},{"name":"Elasmosaurus (long-necked plesiosaurs)","common":"Long-necked plesiosaurs","appear_ma":80,"clade":"vertebrate:reptile","emoji":"🦕","blurb":"Elasmosaurs stretched an astonishing neck of over 70 vertebrae to snatch fish, cruising the Western Interior Seaway on four wing-like flippers.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":66,"lat":40,"lng":-100,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":44.35,"plng":-59.99},{"name":"Velociraptor mongoliensis","common":"Velociraptor","appear_ma":75,"clade":"vertebrate:dinosaur","emoji":"🦤","blurb":"A turkey-sized, feathered dromaeosaur from Mongolia's Gobi Desert armed with a large sickle claw — famously fossilized locked in combat with a Protoceratops.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":71,"lat":44,"lng":103.7,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":45.31,"plng":103.14},{"name":"Grasses (earliest)","common":"First grasses","appear_ma":70,"clade":"plant:flower","emoji":"🌾","blurb":"Grass phytoliths found in fossilized dinosaur dung reveal that the grass family had already appeared in the latest Cretaceous, long before grasslands took over the world.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Tyrannosaurus rex","common":"T. rex","appear_ma":68,"clade":"vertebrate:dinosaur","emoji":"🦖","blurb":"The apex predator of latest-Cretaceous North America, a 12-meter, multi-ton carnivore with bone-crushing jaws that lived right up until the asteroid struck.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":66,"lat":47,"lng":-107,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":54.4,"plng":-66.38},{"name":"Triceratops","common":"Triceratops","appear_ma":68,"clade":"vertebrate:dinosaur","emoji":"🦕","blurb":"A three-horned, frilled herbivore the size of an elephant, among the very last non-avian dinosaurs and a favorite prey of T. rex in the Hell Creek badlands.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":66,"lat":47,"lng":-107,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":54.4,"plng":-66.38},{"name":"Quetzalcoatlus northropi","common":"Quetzalcoatlus","appear_ma":68,"clade":"vertebrate:reptile","emoji":"🦅","blurb":"An azhdarchid pterosaur the size of a giraffe with a 10-11 meter wingspan, one of the largest flying animals ever, which likely stalked prey on land like a giant stork.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":66,"lat":29,"lng":-104,"era":"Cretaceous","plat":36.71,"plng":-72.3},{"name":"Purgatorius","common":"Earliest primate relative","appear_ma":66,"clade":"mammal:primate","emoji":"🐿️","blurb":"A tiny, squirrel-like plesiadapiform from the earliest Paleocene of Montana, known mainly from teeth and ankle bones suited to grasping branches. It sits near the base of the primate family tree.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":63,"lat":47.5,"lng":-106.9,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":54.83,"plng":-65.92},{"name":"Titanoboa cerrejonensis","common":"Giant snake","appear_ma":60,"clade":"vertebrate:reptile","emoji":"🐍","blurb":"The largest snake ever known, stretching some 13 meters and weighing over a tonne, it prowled the steamy Paleocene rainforests of Colombia. Its size reflects the intense tropical heat that followed the dinosaurs.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":58,"lat":9.5,"lng":-72.6,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":3.06,"plng":-53.57},{"name":"Eohippus (Hyracotherium)","common":"Dawn horse","appear_ma":52,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🐴","blurb":"A dog-sized forest browser with multiple toes and low-crowned teeth, it stands at the root of the horse lineage. Over 50 million years its descendants grew larger, lost toes, and evolved grazing teeth.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":45,"lat":41.5,"lng":-109,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":45.81,"plng":-81.02},{"name":"Pakicetus","common":"First whale","appear_ma":50,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🐺","blurb":"A wolf-sized, four-legged land mammal from Pakistan with an ear region marking it as the earliest known whale, likely wading in shallow streams. It launched the extraordinary return of mammals to the sea.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":48,"lat":32.5,"lng":72,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":7.83,"plng":68.81},{"name":"Darwinius masillae ('Ida')","common":"Eocene primate","appear_ma":47,"clade":"mammal:primate","emoji":"🐒","blurb":"An exquisitely preserved early primate from Germany's Messel Pit, complete with fur outline and gut contents of leaves and fruit. It captures the early radiation of primates in Eocene forests.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":47,"lat":49.92,"lng":8.76,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":39.72,"plng":11.41},{"name":"Uintatherium","common":"Horned Eocene giant","appear_ma":45,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🦏","blurb":"A rhino-sized herbivore bristling with six bony knobs on its skull and dagger-like canines, it browsed Eocene North America and Asia. It belonged to an early, now-vanished branch of hoofed mammals.","importance":2,"extinct_ma":37,"lat":40.5,"lng":-109.5,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":43.93,"plng":-86.48},{"name":"Andrewsarchus","common":"Giant predatory ungulate","appear_ma":41,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🐗","blurb":"Known from a single huge skull from Mongolia, this Eocene beast had one of the largest carnivorous mammal heads ever found. It was actually a relative of hoofed mammals, not a true carnivore.","importance":2,"extinct_ma":35,"lat":44,"lng":104,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":46.12,"plng":96.89},{"name":"Basilosaurus","common":"Fully aquatic early whale","appear_ma":40,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🐋","blurb":"A serpentine, 18-meter fully marine whale from Eocene Egypt and the U.S., with tiny vestigial hind legs revealing its land-dwelling ancestry. Its fossils fill the famous 'Valley of the Whales' at Wadi al-Hitan.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":34,"lat":29.27,"lng":30.04,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":15.55,"plng":29.78},{"name":"Paraceratherium","common":"Largest land mammal","appear_ma":34,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🦣","blurb":"A hornless rhinoceros relative from Oligocene Asia standing nearly 5 meters at the shoulder and weighing up to 20 tonnes, it browsed treetops like a giraffe. It is among the largest land mammals ever to live.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":23,"lat":45,"lng":90,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":46.84,"plng":84.38},{"name":"Grasses (Poaceae) radiation","common":"Grasses","appear_ma":34,"clade":"plant:flower","emoji":"🌾","blurb":"Though grasses arose earlier, they diversified explosively in the cooling Oligocene and Miocene to carpet open landscapes. Their tough, silica-laden blades reshaped herbivore teeth and whole ecosystems.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":null,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Proconsul","common":"Early ape","appear_ma":21,"clade":"mammal:primate","emoji":"🦍","blurb":"A tailless, tree-dwelling early ape from Miocene East Africa, combining monkey-like and ape-like traits as it moved cautiously through the canopy. It illuminates the dawn of the ape lineage that leads to us.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":17,"lat":-0.4,"lng":34.2,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":-6.07,"plng":34.4},{"name":"Gomphotherium / early elephants","common":"Early elephants","appear_ma":20,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🐘","blurb":"Four-tusked proboscideans that spread across Miocene Africa and Eurasia, part of the great elephant radiation from Africa's Paleogene ancestors. They gave rise to the mastodons, mammoths, and modern elephants.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":1.5,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Moa","appear_ma":8,"clade":"vertebrate:bird","emoji":"🐦","blurb":"Giant flightless birds of New Zealand, the tallest species towered over three metres, browsing forests that had no mammals. Māori hunting drove all nine species to extinction within a couple of centuries of human arrival.","importance":4,"lat":-43.5,"lng":172,"extinct_ma":0.0006,"common":"moa","era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":-42.08,"plng":179.34},{"name":"Sahelanthropus tchadensis","common":"Earliest hominin","appear_ma":7,"clade":"mammal:hominin","emoji":"🦴","blurb":"Known from a distorted skull ('Toumaï') from Chad, its forward-set foramen magnum hints at upright walking around the time our lineage split from chimpanzees. It is among the oldest known members of the human family.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":6,"lat":16,"lng":18,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":10.78,"plng":17.77},{"name":"Mammuthus (mammoths)","common":"Mammoths","appear_ma":5,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🦣","blurb":"Emerging in Africa around 5 million years ago and spreading across Eurasia and North America, mammoths grew shaggy coats and curved tusks to thrive on cold grasslands. They became icons of the coming ice ages.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":0.004,"lat":null,"lng":null,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":null,"plng":null},{"name":"Ardipithecus ramidus ('Ardi')","common":"Early hominin","appear_ma":4.4,"clade":"mammal:hominin","emoji":"🦶","blurb":"A 4.4-million-year-old Ethiopian hominin with a grasping big toe yet an upright-adapted pelvis, it walked bipedally on the ground while still climbing trees. It reveals that our ancestors' last common ancestor with apes was not chimp-like.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":4.2,"lat":10.5,"lng":40.5,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":10.5,"plng":40.5},{"name":"Australopithecus afarensis ('Lucy')","common":"Lucy's species","appear_ma":3.9,"clade":"mammal:hominin","emoji":"🚶","blurb":"The famous 3.2-million-year-old 'Lucy' skeleton from Ethiopia's Afar region, and the Laetoli footprints, prove committed upright walking with a still-small, ape-sized brain. It is a keystone of the human story.","importance":5,"extinct_ma":2.9,"lat":11.1,"lng":40.6,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":11.1,"plng":40.6},{"name":"Smilodon / sabre-tooth cats","common":"Sabre-tooth cats","appear_ma":2.5,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🐯","blurb":"Machairodont cats wielding elongated canine daggers evolved to kill large prey, culminating in the powerfully built Smilodon of the Americas. Sabre-toothed forms recur repeatedly across the age of mammals.","importance":4,"extinct_ma":0.01,"lat":34.06,"lng":-118.36,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":34.06,"plng":-118.36},{"name":"Smilodon","appear_ma":2.5,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🐯","blurb":"The sabre-toothed cat wielded 18-centimetre canines to bring down bison and young mammoths in the Americas. Thousands were trapped and preserved in the tar of La Brea in Los Angeles.","importance":4,"lat":34.06,"lng":-118.36,"extinct_ma":0.01,"common":"sabre-toothed cat","era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":34.06,"plng":-118.36},{"name":"Homo habilis","appear_ma":2.3,"clade":"mammal:hominin","emoji":"🪨","blurb":"One of the earliest members of our genus, the 'handy man' was a small-brained tool-user in East Africa who flaked simple stone choppers. It butchered scavenged carcasses on the ancient African savanna.","importance":4,"lat":-2.99,"lng":35.35,"extinct_ma":1.5,"common":"handy man","era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":-2.99,"plng":35.35},{"name":"Megatherium (giant ground sloth)","common":"Giant ground sloth","appear_ma":2,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🦥","blurb":"An elephant-sized ground sloth of South America that reared up to browse trees with massive clawed forelimbs. It was one of the megafauna that trekked north during the Great American Biotic Interchange.","importance":3,"extinct_ma":0.011,"lat":-34,"lng":-58,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene","plat":-34.0,"plng":-58.0},{"name":"Homo erectus","appear_ma":2,"clade":"mammal:hominin","emoji":"🔥","blurb":"The first truly human-bodied ancestor, Homo erectus walked with long striding legs, tamed fire, and was the first hominin to leave Africa, spreading across Asia to Java and China. It endured for well over a million years, longer than any other human species.","importance":5,"lat":4,"lng":36,"extinct_ma":0.11,"common":"upright man","era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":4.0,"plng":36.0},{"name":"Megatherium","appear_ma":2,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🦥","blurb":"A ground sloth the size of an elephant, Megatherium reared up on its hind legs to strip leaves from trees across South America. It was among the largest land mammals ever, and among the megafauna that vanished as humans spread.","importance":4,"lat":-34.6,"lng":-58.4,"extinct_ma":0.011,"common":"giant ground sloth","era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":-34.6,"plng":-58.4},{"name":"Diprotodon","appear_ma":1.6,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🐻","blurb":"The largest marsupial that ever lived, a wombat-like giant the size of a hippo that browsed across Ice Age Australia. It disappeared soon after people arrived on the continent.","importance":4,"lat":-30,"lng":142,"extinct_ma":0.04,"common":"giant wombat","era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":-30.0,"plng":142.0},{"name":"Dodo","appear_ma":1,"clade":"vertebrate:bird","emoji":"🦤","blurb":"A flightless pigeon the size of a turkey, evolved without predators on Mauritius and utterly unafraid of the sailors who arrived. Within a century of European contact it was gone, becoming the enduring emblem of human-caused extinction.","importance":4,"lat":-20.2,"lng":57.5,"extinct_ma":3e-05,"common":"dodo","era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":-20.2,"plng":57.5},{"name":"Homo heidelbergensis","appear_ma":0.7,"clade":"mammal:hominin","emoji":"🗿","blurb":"A big-brained species that hunted large game with wooden spears in Ice Age Europe and Africa, likely the common ancestor of Neanderthals and us. Its fossils span from Spain's 'Pit of Bones' to Zambia.","importance":3,"lat":49.4,"lng":8.7,"extinct_ma":0.2,"common":"Heidelberg man","era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":49.4,"plng":8.7},{"name":"Cave lion","appear_ma":0.6,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🦁","blurb":"A huge maneless lion that stalked the Ice Age steppes of Eurasia and appeared in the earliest cave art. Frozen cubs recovered from Siberian permafrost still bear their fur.","importance":3,"lat":45.05,"lng":3.55,"extinct_ma":0.013,"common":"cave lion","era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":45.05,"plng":3.55},{"name":"Homo neanderthalensis","appear_ma":0.4,"clade":"mammal:hominin","emoji":"🧔","blurb":"Cold-adapted, powerfully built cousins who dominated Ice Age Europe and the Near East, made fine tools, buried their dead and painted caves. They interbred with our ancestors, and their genes live on in most people alive today.","importance":5,"lat":50.9,"lng":7.1,"extinct_ma":0.04,"common":"Neanderthal","era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":50.9,"plng":7.1},{"name":"Homo sapiens (Denisovans)","appear_ma":0.4,"clade":"mammal:hominin","emoji":"🦴","blurb":"A ghost lineage known mainly from DNA in a Siberian cave, Denisovans ranged across Asia and interbred with modern humans. Their genes still help Tibetans breathe at high altitude today.","importance":4,"lat":51.4,"lng":84.7,"extinct_ma":0.03,"common":"Denisovans","era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":51.4,"plng":84.7},{"name":"Woolly mammoth","appear_ma":0.4,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🦣","blurb":"The shaggy, curl-tusked icon of the Ice Age grazed the vast mammoth steppe from Spain to the Yukon. Most died out as the ice retreated, but a relict herd survived on Wrangel Island until about 4,000 years ago.","importance":5,"lat":71.2,"lng":-179.4,"extinct_ma":0.004,"common":"woolly mammoth","era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":71.2,"plng":-179.4},{"name":"Woolly rhinoceros","appear_ma":0.35,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🦏","blurb":"A two-horned, fur-covered rhino that grazed the frozen steppe alongside mammoths, using its front horn to sweep snow off grass. Deep-frozen carcasses have emerged intact from Siberian permafrost.","importance":3,"lat":67.5,"lng":133.4,"extinct_ma":0.01,"common":"woolly rhino","era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":67.5,"plng":133.4},{"name":"Homo sapiens","appear_ma":0.3,"clade":"mammal:hominin","emoji":"🧠","blurb":"Our own species arose in Africa around 300,000 years ago, its earliest fossils found at Jebel Irhoud in Morocco. Behavioural modernity, symbolic art and language let us spread out of Africa and around the entire planet.","importance":5,"lat":31.9,"lng":-8.9,"extinct_ma":null,"common":"modern humans","era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":31.9,"plng":-8.9},{"name":"Dire wolf","appear_ma":0.25,"clade":"vertebrate:mammal","emoji":"🐺","blurb":"A heavier, bone-crushing relative of the grey wolf that hunted in packs across the Americas. Its bones are the most abundant mammal preserved in the La Brea tar pits.","importance":3,"lat":34.06,"lng":-118.36,"extinct_ma":0.01,"common":"dire wolf","era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":34.06,"plng":-118.36},{"name":"Homo floresiensis","appear_ma":0.1,"clade":"mammal:hominin","emoji":"🧍","blurb":"The 'hobbit' of Flores stood barely a metre tall, a dwarfed human species isolated on an Indonesian island where it hunted pygmy elephants. It survived until roughly 50,000 years ago.","importance":3,"lat":-8.6,"lng":121.1,"extinct_ma":0.05,"common":"the Hobbit","era":"Quaternary-Humans","plat":-8.6,"plng":121.1}];

const EXTINCTIONS = [{"name":"The Oxygen Catastrophe (first mass extinction)","ma":2400,"cause":"Free molecular oxygen from cyanobacterial photosynthesis, a corrosive poison to anaerobic life, flooded the atmosphere and oceans","blurb":"Earth's first great die-off struck the anaerobic microbes that had dominated for over a billion years, as oxygen wrecked their oxygen-sensitive enzymes and metabolisms. Survivors were driven into permanent refuge in muds, deep waters, and hydrothermal settings, where their descendants live on.","pct":null,"importance":5,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian"},{"name":"Post-Lomagundi biosphere collapse","ma":2060,"cause":"Crash in atmospheric oxygen and nutrient supply following the end of the Lomagundi-Jatuli carbon isotope excursion","blurb":"The abrupt end of the great carbon-burial event coincided with a probable plunge in oxygen and primary productivity, ushering in a long, sluggish low-oxygen interval. Organic-rich shales of the aftermath record a biosphere pushed back toward the edge.","pct":null,"importance":3,"era":"Siderian-Rhyacian"},{"name":"Lomagundi biosphere overshoot collapse","ma":2060,"cause":"Crash in productivity and oxygen after the Lomagundi carbon-isotope excursion, as the pulse of buried organic carbon exhausted nutrient supplies","blurb":"The great burst of photosynthetic productivity that drove the Lomagundi excursion appears to have overshot its nutrient limits and collapsed, dragging oxygen back down. This bust may have culled oxygen-dependent microbes and helped usher in the long, low-oxygen mid-Proterozoic.","importance":3,"pct":null,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian"},{"name":"Banded-iron microbial community decline","ma":1850,"cause":"Exhaustion of dissolved ferrous iron in the oceans and shift to sulphidic waters","blurb":"As the last banded iron formations precipitated and the seas turned sulphidic, the iron-oxidising microbial communities that had thrived for over a billion years lost their chemical fuel and dwindled. Their decline marks the end of an iron-dominated ocean.","importance":2,"pct":null,"era":"Orosirian-Statherian"},{"name":"Late Tonian acritarch turnover","ma":740,"cause":"Environmental change and cooling as Rodinia rifted and the climate slid toward Cryogenian glaciation, disrupting stable planktic communities.","blurb":"Toward the end of the Tonian, several long-lived large ornamented acritarch and stromatolite assemblages decline and disappear as the placid Boring Billion conditions break down. It is a gradual biotic reshuffling rather than a sharp mass extinction, foreshadowing the upheavals of the Cryogenian.","importance":2,"pct":null,"era":"BoringBillion"},{"name":"Kotlin/Nama assemblage turnover","ma":550,"cause":"Environmental change and ecological restructuring","blurb":"The classic White Sea Ediacaran communities declined and were replaced by the skeletal Nama assemblage, an earlier faunal turnover that thinned the ranks of the frondose Ediacarans before their final disappearance.","importance":3,"pct":null,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran"},{"name":"Ediacaran biotic decline (end-Ediacaran extinction)","ma":539,"cause":"Biological displacement and environmental change","blurb":"As mobile, burrowing, skeletonised animals spread at the Ediacaran-Cambrian boundary, the soft-bodied Ediacaran organisms vanished - possibly out-competed, eaten, or 'engineered' out of existence as animals churned the seafloor and mats they depended on.","importance":4,"pct":null,"era":"Cryogenian-Ediacaran"},{"name":"End-Botomian / Cambrian extinctions","ma":513,"cause":"Ocean anoxia and possible volcanic disruption drove repeated Cambrian crises, including the collapse of archaeocyathid reefs and losses among trilobites.","blurb":"A series of early-to-middle Cambrian extinction pulses repeatedly cut down trilobite and reef communities, resetting marine ecosystems.","importance":2,"pct":null,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician"},{"name":"Ordovician-Silurian mass extinction","ma":443.8,"cause":"Rapid Gondwanan glaciation crashed sea levels and cooled the oceans, followed by an equally lethal warming and anoxia when the ice melted.","blurb":"The first of the Big Five mass extinctions wiped out roughly 85% of marine species in two pulses tied to the onset and end of a severe ice age.","importance":5,"pct":85,"era":"Cambrian-Ordovician"},{"name":"Kellwasser event (Frasnian-Famennian)","ma":372,"cause":"Widespread ocean anoxia and cooling, likely tied to the spread of land plants drawing down CO2 and fertilizing the seas","blurb":"Two pulses of oxygen-starved water laid down black shales and annihilated the tropical reef ecosystems, killing off most corals and stromatoporoids. It was the most severe pulse of the protracted Late Devonian mass extinction.","importance":5,"pct":70,"era":"Silurian-Devonian"},{"name":"Hangenberg event (end-Devonian)","ma":359,"cause":"Renewed marine anoxia combined with a short, sharp glaciation and sea-level swings","blurb":"This terminal Devonian crisis exterminated the armored placoderms outright and severely thinned early vertebrate and ammonoid diversity, ushering in a depauperate recovery interval known as Romer's Gap. It reshuffled the vertebrate world before the Age of Amphibians.","importance":4,"pct":50,"era":"Silurian-Devonian"},{"name":"Carboniferous Rainforest Collapse","ma":305,"cause":"Abrupt aridification and warming that fragmented the equatorial coal-swamp rainforests","blurb":"Not a classic mass extinction but a major ecological upheaval: the continuous lycopsid coal forests broke into isolated pockets, driving many swamp amphibians extinct while favoring reptiles, seed plants and tree ferns that could cope with drier conditions.","importance":4,"pct":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian"},{"name":"Olson's Extinction","ma":273,"cause":"Middle Permian environmental stress and turnover among terrestrial synapsids","blurb":"A poorly understood but significant decline in land vertebrate diversity in the mid-Permian, thinning the ranks of pelycosaur-grade synapsids well before the end-Permian catastrophe.","importance":2,"pct":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian"},{"name":"End-Guadalupian (Capitanian) extinction","ma":259,"cause":"Emeishan Traps volcanism in China, ocean anoxia and cooling","blurb":"A distinct extinction pulse late in the middle Permian, linked to the Emeishan flood basalts, that hit reef builders, fusulinids and dicynodonts and served as a prelude to the far greater end-Permian collapse.","importance":3,"pct":null,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian"},{"name":"Permian-Triassic Extinction (the Great Dying)","ma":251.9,"cause":"Siberian Traps flood volcanism causing extreme greenhouse warming, ocean anoxia and acidification","blurb":"The most catastrophic extinction in Earth's history, erasing about 90-96% of marine species and roughly 70% of land vertebrate families and ending the reign of trilobites, rugose corals, fusulinids and most Paleozoic life; it closed the Paleozoic Era and cleared the stage for the dinosaurs.","importance":5,"pct":95,"era":"Carboniferous-Permian"},{"name":"Smithian-Spathian extinction","ma":249.5,"cause":"Extreme greenhouse heat and ocean anoxia during the Early Triassic recovery","blurb":"A lesser-known Early Triassic crisis in which lethally hot equatorial oceans repeatedly hammered the fragile post-Permian ecosystems, delaying full recovery. Marine life could not stabilise until conditions finally cooled.","importance":2,"pct":null,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic"},{"name":"Carnian extinction pulse","ma":233,"cause":"Wrangellia volcanism and the humid Carnian Pluvial Episode","blurb":"A turnover event at the Carnian humid interval reshuffled marine invertebrates and terrestrial faunas, clearing ecological space in which dinosaurs and modern conifers rose to prominence.","importance":3,"pct":null,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic"},{"name":"Triassic-Jurassic mass extinction","ma":201.4,"cause":"CAMP flood-basalt volcanism, CO2-driven warming and ocean acidification","blurb":"One of the 'Big Five,' this extinction wiped out roughly half of marine genera and devastated large amphibians and crocodile-line archosaurs on land. Its clearing of competitors opened the way for dinosaurs to dominate the Jurassic.","importance":5,"pct":50,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic"},{"name":"Pliensbachian-Toarcian (Early Jurassic) extinction","ma":183,"cause":"Karoo-Ferrar volcanism, ocean anoxia and warming (Toarcian anoxic event)","blurb":"A pulse of Gondwanan volcanism drove a warming spike and widespread seafloor oxygen loss in the Early Jurassic, hitting ammonites, bivalves, and other marine life. It left black, organic-rich shales across the world's oceans.","importance":3,"pct":null,"era":"Triassic-Jurassic"},{"name":"Cenomanian-Turonian extinction (OAE 2)","ma":94,"cause":"Widespread ocean anoxia and warming linked to volcanic CO2, choking oxygen from seafloor waters","blurb":"A mid-Cretaceous marine crisis driven by ocean anoxia wiped out roughly a quarter of marine invertebrate genera, hitting ammonites, bivalves and reef ecosystems hard.","importance":3,"pct":null,"era":"Cretaceous"},{"name":"Cretaceous-Paleogene (K-Pg) mass extinction","ma":66,"cause":"Chicxulub asteroid impact causing global impact winter, wildfires and acid rain, compounded by Deccan Traps volcanism","blurb":"The fifth great mass extinction eliminated all non-avian dinosaurs, pterosaurs, mosasaurs, plesiosaurs and ammonites, killing about three-quarters of all species and clearing the stage for mammals to inherit the Earth.","importance":5,"pct":75,"era":"Cretaceous"},{"name":"Grande Coupure (Eocene-Oligocene extinction)","ma":34,"cause":"Rapid global cooling and Antarctic glaciation at the Eocene-Oligocene boundary, drying and opening habitats and shifting faunas via new land connections.","blurb":"As the world flipped from hothouse to icehouse, many archaic Eocene mammal lineages in Europe and elsewhere were wiped out and replaced by Asian immigrants. Forest browsers gave way to animals of drier, open country.","importance":4,"pct":null,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene"},{"name":"Late Miocene faunal turnover","ma":7,"cause":"Global cooling and drying with the spread of C4 grasslands, restructuring herbivore communities toward grazers.","blurb":"As savannas expanded, browsing forest mammals declined while grazing herbivores and their predators diversified. The reorganization set the ecological stage on which early hominins appeared in Africa.","importance":2,"pct":null,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene"},{"name":"South American endemic mammal decline","ma":2,"cause":"Competition and predation from North American placentals arriving via the Panama land bridge during the Great American Biotic Interchange.","blurb":"Many of South America's long-isolated native mammals, including marsupial predators and strange hoofed forms, dwindled as northern cats, dogs, and bears moved in. The interchange proved lopsided against the southern fauna.","importance":3,"pct":null,"era":"Cenozoic-Paleogene-Neogene"},{"name":"Quaternary megafauna extinction","ma":0.013,"cause":"Human hunting combined with rapid post-glacial climate change","blurb":"As modern humans spread across the globe and the ice retreated, most of the world's giant land animals vanished, mammoths, ground sloths, giant kangaroos, moa and Diprotodon among them. The losses were heaviest exactly where humans newly arrived, in Australia and the Americas.","importance":5,"pct":null,"era":"Quaternary-Humans"},{"name":"Holocene / Anthropocene extinction (Sixth Mass Extinction)","ma":0,"cause":"Human activity: habitat loss, hunting, invasive species, pollution and climate change","blurb":"The ongoing biodiversity collapse is driven entirely by one species, with extinction rates estimated at tens to hundreds of times the natural background. Icons already lost, the dodo, great auk, thylacine and passenger pigeon, are the visible edge of a far broader unravelling of life.","importance":5,"pct":null,"era":"Quaternary-Humans"}];
